Ava | 72000']::text[], 'SELECT name, salary FROM employees ORDER BY salary DESC LIMIT 2', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["name","salary"],"rows":[["Ava",72000],["Ben",58000],["Cy",91000],["Dee",64000],["Eli",85000]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ORDER BY is applied before LIMIT: salaries are sorted highest first, then only the top 2 rows are kept.', array['employees']::text[]),
(34, 'easy', 'multiple-choice', 'ORDER BY', 'Which query lists products from most to least expensive?', 'SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY price DESC', '{}'::text[], array['SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY price DESC', 'SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY price', 'SELECT * FROM products SORT BY price DESC', 'SELECT * FROM products ORDER BY DESC price']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The direction keyword comes after the column: ORDER BY price DESC. Without DESC the sort is ascending, and SORT BY isn''t valid SQL.', array['products']::text[]),
(35, 'easy', 'write-sql', 'DISTINCT', 'Which order statuses are actually in use? List each status once.', 'SELECT DISTINCT status FROM orders;', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Remove duplicate rows from the result.', 'DISTINCT collapses repeated values, turning thousands of orders into a short list of statuses. It''s a quick way to explore which values a column holds.', array['orders']::text[]),
(36, 'easy', 'drag-drop', 'WHERE', 'Arrange the pieces to show the name and stock of products with fewer than 10 in stock.', 'SELECT name, stock FROM products WHERE stock < 10', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name, stock', 'FROM products', 'WHERE stock < 10']::text[], array['HAVING stock < 10']::text[], null, 'Row filters go in WHERE. HAVING filters groups after GROUP BY, so it''s the wrong tool here.', array['products']::text[]),
(37, 'easy', 'multiple-choice', 'LIMIT', 'No ORDER BY: which rows does SELECT * FROM orders LIMIT 5 return?', 'Any 5 rows, order not guaranteed', '{}'::text[], array['Any 5 rows, order not guaranteed', 'The first 5 rows ever inserted', 'The 5 rows with the lowest ids', 'The 5 most recent orders']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Without ORDER BY, SQL guarantees no row order, so LIMIT keeps whichever 5 rows come first. Add ORDER BY whenever you need a specific 5.', array['orders']::text[]),
