ORDER BY department', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["department","age"],"rows":[["Finance",30],["Finance",40],["Finance",null],["Marketing",28]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'AVG ignores NULLs entirely, so Finance averages only 30 and 40: 35. Treating the NULL as 0 would give 23.33.', array['employees']::text[]),
(176, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'INNER JOIN', 'Where does everyone work? Inner join employees e to departments d (employees first) and return e.name and d.location.', 'SELECT e.name, d.location FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.department = d.name', array['SELECT e.name, d.location FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON d.name = e.department']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'These tables are linked by department name, not by id.', 'A join key doesn''t have to be an id: employees.department stores the department''s name, so ON e.department = d.name links each employee to their department''s row.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(177, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'AVG', 'Why does this query fail?', 'Aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE', '{}'::text[], array['Aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE', 'AVG only works on integer columns', 'name must be listed in a GROUP BY', 'WHERE cannot compare two numeric values']::text[], 'SELECT name FROM employees
WHERE salary > AVG(salary)', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'WHERE is evaluated row by row before any aggregation, so AVG isn''t available there. To compare with the overall average, use a subquery: WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees).', array['employees']::text[]),
(178, 'medium', 'fix-query', 'INNER JOIN', 'This should list each employee''s name with their department''s budget, but PostgreSQL complains. Fix it.', 'SELECT e.name, d.budget FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.department = d.name', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, d.budget FROM employees e JOIN departments d ON e.department = d.name', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Which tables have a column called name?', 'Both employees and departments have a name column, so an unqualified name is ambiguous. Prefix it with the alias (e.name) to say which one you mean.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(179, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'INNER JOIN', 'Revenue by customer tier: inner join customers c to orders o (customers first) and return c.tier and the sum of o.total_amount aliased revenue. Prefix every column.', 'SELECT c.tier, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.tier', array['SELECT c.tier, SUM(o.total_amount) AS revenue FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id GROUP BY c.tier']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Join first, then group by a column from the joined result.', 'The join attaches each order to its customer''s tier; GROUP BY c.tier then sums the order amounts per tier. Aggregates work on joined rows just like on a single table.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(180, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'INNER JOIN', 'Customer 7 has 3 orders. After customers INNER JOIN orders ON customers.id = orders.customer_id, how many result rows belong to customer 7?', '3', '{}'::text[], array['3', '1', '4', '0']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A join outputs one row per matching pair. Customer 7''s single row pairs with each of its 3 orders, so the customer''s details repeat 3 times.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(181, 'medium', 'drag-drop', 'INNER JOIN', 'Count products per supplier country, most products first.', 'SELECT s.country, COUNT(*) AS product_count FROM products p JOIN suppliers s ON p.supplier_id = s.id GROUP BY s.country ORDER BY product_count DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT s.country, COUNT(*) AS product_count', 'FROM products p', 'JOIN suppliers s ON p.supplier_id = s.id', 'GROUP BY s.country', 'ORDER BY product_count DESC']::text[], array['GROUP BY p.supplier_id']::text[], null, 'After joining, each product row carries its supplier''s country, so GROUP BY s.country counts products per country. Grouping by supplier_id would leave s.country ungrouped.', array['products', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(182, 'medium', 'predict-output', 'INNER JOIN', 'Finance has no row in departments. What does this return?', 'Berlin | 2
London | 2', '{}'::text[], array['Berlin | 2
London | 2', 'Berlin | 1
London | 2', 'Berlin | 2
London | 2
NULL | 1', 'Berlin | 2
London | 1
London | 1']::text[], 'SELECT d.location, COUNT(*) AS staff
FROM employees e
JOIN departments d ON e.department = d.name
GROUP BY d.location
ORDER BY d.location', '[{"name":"departments","columns":["name","location"],"rows":[["Sales","London"],["Engineering","Berlin"],["HR","London"]]},{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department"],"rows":[["Ana","Sales"],["Ben","Engineering"],["Cy","Engineering"],["Dee","HR"],["Eve","Finance"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Eve''s Finance row has no match, so the inner join drops her. The rest are grouped by location: Ben and Cy in Berlin, Ana (Sales) and Dee (HR) in London.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(183, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'LEFT JOIN', 'Find customers who have never placed an order. Left join customers c to orders o and return only c.name.', 'SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.id IS NULL', array['SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.id IS NULL', 'SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Unmatched rows get NULLs in the orders columns.', 'After a LEFT JOIN, customers without orders carry NULLs in every orders column, so WHERE o.id IS NULL keeps exactly those. Use IS NULL: = NULL is never true.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(184, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'INNER JOIN', 'Which ON condition correctly joins employees e with departments d?', 'ON e.department = d.name', '{}'::text[], array['ON e.department = d.name', 'ON e.id = d.id', 'ON e.name = d.name', 'ON e.department = d.id']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'employees.department stores the department''s name, so it must match departments.name. Matching ids pairs unrelated rows, and comparing text to an integer id is an error.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(185, 'medium', 'fix-query', 'LEFT JOIN', 'This should show each department''s headcount with 0 for departments that have no employees, but empty departments show 1. Fix it.', 'SELECT d.name, COUNT(e.id) AS headcount FROM departments d LEFT JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department GROUP BY d.name', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT d.name, COUNT(*) AS headcount FROM departments d LEFT JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department GROUP BY d.name', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'COUNT(*) counts rows, even NULL-padded ones.', 'An empty department still produces one LEFT JOIN row whose employee columns are NULL. COUNT(*) counts that row; COUNT(e.id) skips the NULL and returns 0.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(186, 'medium', 'drag-drop', 'LEFT JOIN', 'List every supplier with its products, including suppliers with no products yet, sorted by supplier name.', 'SELECT s.name AS supplier, p.name AS product FROM suppliers s LEFT JOIN products p ON s.id = p.supplier_id ORDER BY s.name', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT s.name AS supplier, p.name AS product', 'FROM suppliers s', 'LEFT JOIN products p', 'ON s.id = p.supplier_id', 'ORDER BY s.name']::text[], array['INNER JOIN products p']::text[], null, 'LEFT JOIN keeps every supplier; those without products appear once with a NULL product. An INNER JOIN would silently drop them.', array['products', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(187, 'medium', 'predict-output', 'LEFT JOIN', 'What does this per-supplier count return?', 'Acme | 2
Bolt | 0
Crest | 1', '{}'::text[], array['Acme | 2
Bolt | 0
Crest | 1', 'Acme | 2
Bolt | 1
Crest | 1', 'Acme | 2
Crest | 1', 'Acme | 2
Bolt | NULL
Crest | 1']::text[], 'SELECT s.name, COUNT(p.id) AS n
FROM suppliers s
LEFT JOIN products p ON s.id = p.supplier_id
GROUP BY s.name
ORDER BY s.name', '[{"name":"suppliers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[[1,"Acme"],[2,"Bolt"],[3,"Crest"]]},{"name":"products","columns":["id","supplier_id"],"rows":[[1,1],[2,1],[3,3]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Bolt has no products, so the LEFT JOIN gives it one row with p.id NULL. COUNT(p.id) skips that NULL and returns 0; COUNT never returns NULL.', array['products', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(188, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'LEFT JOIN', 'Count orders for every customer, showing 0 for customers with none. Join customers c (first) to orders o; return c.name and the count aliased order_count. Prefix every column.', 'SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.name', array['SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id GROUP BY c.name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Which COUNT form gives 0 for a customer''s unmatched row?', 'A customer with no orders still produces one row, with o.id NULL. COUNT(o.id) ignores that NULL and returns 0, whereas COUNT(*) would count the row and return 1.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(189, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'LEFT JOIN', 'Customers without orders are missing from this result. Why?', 'WHERE o.status = ''shipped'' discards rows where o is NULL', '{}'::text[], array['WHERE o.status = ''shipped'' discards rows where o is NULL', 'LEFT JOIN drops unmatched rows by default', 'customers must be the second table in a LEFT JOIN', 'o.status must also appear in the SELECT list']::text[], 'SELECT c.name, o.id
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.status = ''shipped''', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Unmatched customers get NULL for o.status, and NULL = ''shipped'' isn''t true, so WHERE throws them away and the LEFT JOIN acts like an INNER JOIN. Move the condition into ON to keep them.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(190, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'HAVING', 'Considering only ''shipped'' orders, which customers'' total_amount adds up to more than 1000? Return customer_id and the sum aliased total_spent.', 'SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS total_spent FROM orders WHERE status = ''shipped'' GROUP BY customer_id HAVING SUM(total_amount) > 1000', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One filter is per row, the other is per group.', 'status = ''shipped'' tests individual rows, so it belongs in WHERE (before grouping). The 1000 threshold tests each customer''s total, so it belongs in HAVING (after grouping).', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(191, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'HAVING', 'This query has HAVING but no GROUP BY. What does PostgreSQL do?', 'Treats all rows as one group; returns 1 row or 0 rows', '{}'::text[], array['Treats all rows as one group; returns 1 row or 0 rows', 'Raises an error: HAVING requires GROUP BY', 'Checks the condition row by row, like WHERE', 'Always returns one row and ignores HAVING']::text[], 'SELECT COUNT(*) FROM orders
HAVING COUNT(*) > 100', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Without GROUP BY, an aggregate query treats the whole table as a single group. HAVING then keeps that one row if COUNT(*) > 100, or returns no rows at all.', array['orders']::text[]),
(192, 'medium', 'fix-query', 'HAVING', 'This should list customers whose average order is above 250, but PostgreSQL says a column doesn''t exist. Fix it.', 'SELECT customer_id, AVG(total_amount) AS avg_order FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING AVG(total_amount) > 250', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT customer_id, AVG(total_amount) AS avg_order FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING avg_order > 250', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'HAVING is evaluated before SELECT aliases exist.', 'HAVING is evaluated before the SELECT list, so the alias avg_order isn''t visible there in PostgreSQL. Repeat the aggregate expression instead.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(193, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'SUM', 'Which 3 departments have the biggest salary bill? Return department and the total salary aliased payroll, highest first.', 'SELECT department, SUM(salary) AS payroll FROM employees GROUP BY department ORDER BY payroll DESC LIMIT 3', array['SELECT department, SUM(salary) AS payroll FROM employees GROUP BY department ORDER BY SUM(salary) DESC LIMIT 3']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Sort the groups by their total, then keep the top few.', 'ORDER BY runs after SELECT, so it can use the alias payroll (unlike HAVING). LIMIT 3 then keeps the three largest groups.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(194, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'AVG', 'For employees hired on or after ''2020-01-01'', show the average salary per department. Return department and avg_salary, highest average first.', 'SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary FROM employees WHERE hire_date >= ''2020-01-01'' GROUP BY department ORDER BY avg_salary DESC', array['SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary FROM employees WHERE hire_date >= ''2020-01-01'' GROUP BY department ORDER BY AVG(salary) DESC']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Filter rows by date before grouping; sort by the average.', 'The hire-date test applies to rows, so it goes in WHERE before GROUP BY. ORDER BY can use the alias avg_salary because it runs after SELECT.', array['employees']::text[]),
(195, 'medium', 'predict-output', 'LEFT JOIN', 'What does this return?', 'Finance
HR', '{}'::text[], array['Finance
HR', 'Marketing
Sales', '(no rows)', 'Finance
HR
Marketing
Sales']::text[], 'SELECT d.name
FROM departments d
LEFT JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department
WHERE e.name IS NULL
ORDER BY d.name', '[{"name":"departments","columns":["name"],"rows":[["Sales"],["HR"],["Finance"],["Marketing"]]},{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department"],"rows":[["Ana","Sales"],["Ben","Sales"],["Cy","Marketing"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Departments with no employees get NULL employee columns from the LEFT JOIN, and WHERE e.name IS NULL keeps only those. This is the classic ''find rows with no match'' pattern.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(196, 'medium', 'drag-drop', 'HAVING', 'Counting only line items priced over 20, find products that sold more than 100 units in total, best sellers first.', 'SELECT product_id, SUM(quantity) AS units FROM order_items WHERE unit_price > 20 GROUP BY product_id HAVING SUM(quantity) > 100 ORDER BY units DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT product_id, SUM(quantity) AS units', 'FROM order_items', 'WHERE unit_price > 20', 'GROUP BY product_id', 'HAVING SUM(quantity) > 100', 'ORDER BY units DESC']::text[], array['HAVING unit_price > 20', 'WHERE SUM(quantity) > 100']::text[], null, 'The price test is per row (WHERE, before grouping); the units test is per product (HAVING, after grouping). HAVING unit_price would fail because unit_price isn''t grouped.', array['products', 'order_items']::text[]),
(197, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'INNER JOIN', 'Units sold per product: inner join order_items oi to products p (order_items first) and return p.name and the sum of oi.quantity aliased total_qty. Prefix every column.', 'SELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS total_qty FROM order_items oi JOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.id GROUP BY p.name', array['SELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS total_qty FROM order_items oi JOIN products p ON p.id = oi.product_id GROUP BY p.name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Join line items to products, then group by the product name.', 'Each order_items row is matched to its product, then GROUP BY p.name adds up the quantities per product. Products never ordered don''t appear, because an inner join keeps only matches.', array['products', 'order_items']::text[]),
(198, 'medium', 'multiple-choice', 'LEFT JOIN', 'customers has 5 rows and orders has 8. All 8 orders belong to 3 of the customers. How many rows does customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id return?', '10', '{}'::text[], array['10', '8', '5', '13']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The 3 customers with orders produce 8 matched rows, and each of the 2 customers without orders adds one NULL-padded row: 8 + 2 = 10.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(199, 'medium', 'fix-query', 'LEFT JOIN', 'This should show every product''s name with its total units sold (NULL if never ordered). It has two bugs. Fix them.', 'SELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS units_sold FROM products p LEFT JOIN order_items oi ON p.id = oi.product_id GROUP BY p.name', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT p.name, SUM(oi.quantity) AS units_sold FROM products p LEFT JOIN order_items oi p.id = oi.product_id', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Check the join condition and what each output row represents.', 'The join condition needs the ON keyword, and because p.name appears next to an aggregate, the query must GROUP BY p.name to get one row per product.', array['products', 'order_items']::text[]),
(200, 'medium', 'write-sql', 'LEFT JOIN', 'Total salary per department, showing 0 (via COALESCE) for departments with no employees. Join departments d (first) to employees e; return d.name and payroll. Prefix every column.', 'SELECT d.name, COALESCE(SUM(e.salary), 0) AS payroll FROM departments d LEFT JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department GROUP BY d.name', array['SELECT d.name, COALESCE(SUM(e.salary), 0) AS payroll FROM departments d LEFT JOIN employees e ON e.department = d.name GROUP BY d.name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'SUM over nothing but NULLs isn''t 0. Replace the NULL.', 'An empty department joins to a single row of NULLs, and SUM of only NULLs is NULL, not 0. COALESCE(SUM(e.salary), 0) swaps that NULL for 0.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[])
on conflict (id) do nothing;

insert into public.questions (id, difficulty, type, topic, question, answer, alternatives, options, query, sample_tables, tokens, distractors, hint, explanation, schema_tables) values
(201, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Joins', 'The CRM team wants customers who have never ordered. LEFT JOIN customers c to orders o on the customer id and keep only rows where o.id IS NULL. Return c.name.', 'SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.id IS NULL', array['SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE o.id IS NULL', 'SELECT c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id WHERE o.customer_id IS NULL']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Unmatched rows from the right-hand table come back filled with NULLs.', 'This is the anti-join pattern: the LEFT JOIN keeps every customer, and customers with no order get NULL in every o column. Testing a never-NULL right-side column such as o.id with IS NULL keeps only the unmatched ones.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(202, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Window Functions', 'What''s the key difference between GROUP BY department and OVER (PARTITION BY department)?', 'GROUP BY collapses rows; PARTITION BY keeps every row', '{}'::text[], array['GROUP BY collapses rows; PARTITION BY keeps every row', 'PARTITION BY collapses rows; GROUP BY keeps every row', 'No difference: they are synonyms', 'PARTITION BY only works with ROW_NUMBER']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'GROUP BY returns one row per group. A window with PARTITION BY computes per-group values but returns every input row, so detail and group totals can appear side by side.', '{}'::text[]),
(203, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'CASE', 'This should label employees ''senior'' when salary is at least 100000 and ''junior'' otherwise, as level. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN salary >= 100000 THEN ''senior'' ELSE ''junior'' END AS level FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN salary >= 100000 THEN ''senior'' ELSE ''junior'' AS level FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Every CASE needs a closing keyword.', 'A CASE expression must be closed with END before its alias. Without it the parser reaches AS while still inside the CASE and raises a syntax error.', array['employees']::text[]),
(204, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Subqueries', 'Find employees paid above the company-wide average salary, using a scalar subquery in WHERE. Return name, salary.', 'SELECT name, salary FROM employees WHERE salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Aggregates can''t sit directly in WHERE, but a subquery can.', 'A scalar subquery returns exactly one value, so it can be compared with >. Writing WHERE salary > AVG(salary) fails because aggregate functions are not allowed in WHERE.', array['employees']::text[]),
(205, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'UNION', 'What does UNION ALL return here?', 'Oslo
Paris
Paris
Rome
Rome', '{}'::text[], array['Oslo
Paris
Paris
Rome
Rome', 'Oslo
Paris
Rome', 'Paris
Rome
Paris
Rome
Oslo', 'Rome']::text[], 'SELECT city FROM customers
UNION ALL
SELECT city FROM employees
ORDER BY city', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["city"],"rows":[["Paris"],["Rome"],["Paris"]]},{"name":"employees","columns":["city"],"rows":[["Rome"],["Oslo"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'UNION ALL keeps every row from both queries, duplicates included, and the final ORDER BY sorts the combined result. Plain UNION would have returned just Oslo, Paris and Rome.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(206, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'UNION', 'Marketing needs one de-duplicated list of email addresses from customers and employees. Return a single column, email, querying customers first.', 'SELECT email FROM customers UNION SELECT email FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One set operator removes duplicates, the other keeps them.', 'UNION stacks both results and removes duplicate rows, so an address found in both tables appears once. Each SELECT must return the same number of columns with compatible types.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(207, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'CASE', 'Label employees by age as age_group: under 30 ''junior'', under 50 ''mid'', otherwise ''senior''.', 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN age < 30 THEN ''junior'' WHEN age < 50 THEN ''mid'' ELSE ''senior'' END AS age_group FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name,', 'CASE', 'WHEN age < 30 THEN ''junior''', 'WHEN age < 50 THEN ''mid''', 'ELSE ''senior''', 'END AS age_group', 'FROM employees']::text[], array['END CASE AS age_group']::text[], null, 'CASE checks WHENs top to bottom, so the narrower age < 30 test must come before age < 50, or nobody would be ''junior''. The expression closes with END; END CASE is PL/pgSQL syntax, not SQL.', array['employees']::text[]),
(208, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'UNION', 'You know two queries can never return the same row. Which operator should combine them, and why?', 'UNION ALL: it skips the duplicate-removal work', '{}'::text[], array['UNION ALL: it skips the duplicate-removal work', 'UNION: it returns the rows in sorted order', 'UNION: UNION ALL fails when there are no duplicates', 'Either: UNION ALL is just an alias for UNION']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'UNION must de-duplicate the combined rows (by sorting or hashing), while UNION ALL just appends them. When duplicates are impossible, both give the same rows but UNION ALL does less work. Neither guarantees an order without ORDER BY.', '{}'::text[]),
(209, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Window Functions', 'This should rank products by price, most expensive first, as price_rank. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, price, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank FROM products', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, price, RANK() (ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank FROM products', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'A window function needs one keyword before its parentheses.', 'Ranking functions only exist as window functions, so they must be followed by OVER (…). The OVER clause defines the ordering (and optional partitions) for the calculation.', array['products']::text[]),
(210, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'CASE', 'Tag each product with a price_band: ''budget'' under 20, ''mid'' under 100, otherwise ''premium''. Use a searched CASE with the WHENs in that order. Return name, price_band.', 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN price < 20 THEN ''budget'' WHEN price < 100 THEN ''mid'' ELSE ''premium'' END AS price_band FROM products', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'WHEN branches are checked top to bottom; the first match wins.', 'A searched CASE evaluates its WHEN conditions in order and returns the first match, so WHEN price < 100 only ever sees products priced 20 or more. ELSE catches everything left over.', array['products']::text[]),
(211, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Window Functions', 'Finance wants a running total of order amounts in date order. Return order_date, total_amount, and running_total (a SUM window ordered by order_date).', 'SELECT order_date, total_amount, SUM(total_amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date) AS running_total FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'An aggregate with OVER (ORDER BY …) accumulates.', 'With ORDER BY inside OVER, the default frame runs from the first row up to the current row (including rows tied on order_date), so SUM becomes a running total.', array['orders']::text[]),
(212, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'CASE', 'A CASE expression has no ELSE, and none of its WHEN conditions is true for a row. What does it return for that row?', 'NULL', '{}'::text[], array['NULL', '0', 'An empty string', 'An error']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Without ELSE, a CASE in which no WHEN matches returns NULL. Add an ELSE when you need a default such as 0 or ''other''.', '{}'::text[]),
(213, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Joins', 'This should list each order id with its customer''s name, but it errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id', array['SELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON c.id = o.customer_id']::text[], null, 'SELECT o.id, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c WHERE o.customer_id = c.id', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'A JOIN''s match condition lives in its own clause.', 'In PostgreSQL a plain JOIN must be followed by ON (or USING); without it the parser fails at WHERE. The join condition belongs in ON, while WHERE filters the joined rows.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(214, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Joins', 'HR wants each employee listed next to their manager. Self-join employees e to employees m with an inner JOIN. Return e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager.', 'SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager FROM employees e JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id', array['SELECT e.name AS employee, m.name AS manager FROM employees e JOIN employees m ON m.id = e.manager_id']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'An employee''s manager_id points at another row''s id in the same table.', 'A self join gives one table two roles through aliases: e is the report and m is the manager, matched by e.manager_id = m.id. Because it is an inner join, the CEO (manager_id NULL) has no match and drops out.', array['employees']::text[]),
(215, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Subqueries', 'Which kind of subquery is logically re-evaluated for every row of the outer query?', 'A correlated subquery', '{}'::text[], array['A correlated subquery', 'An uncorrelated scalar subquery', 'A derived table in FROM', 'An uncorrelated IN subquery']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A correlated subquery references a column of the outer query, so its result depends on the current outer row and it logically runs once per row. An uncorrelated subquery only needs to be evaluated once (the planner may still rewrite either kind).', '{}'::text[]),
(216, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Subqueries', 'Support wants the names of customers with at least one ''cancelled'' order. Use IN with a subquery on orders. Return name.', 'SELECT name FROM customers WHERE id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''cancelled'')', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The subquery should return a single column of customer ids.', 'IN (subquery) acts as a semi-join: each customer appears at most once, however many cancelled orders they have. A JOIN would repeat the customer once per matching order unless you add DISTINCT.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(217, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Subqueries', 'This should list customers who have at least one order over 1000, but it errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name FROM customers WHERE id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE total_amount > 1000)', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name FROM customers WHERE id IN (SELECT customer_id, total_amount FROM orders WHERE total_amount > 1000)', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One value on the left, so how many columns on the right?', 'A single value compared with IN needs a subquery that returns exactly one column; extra columns cause "subquery has too many columns". The filter column can stay in the subquery''s WHERE without being selected.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(218, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Joins', 'What does this LEFT JOIN return?', 'Ana | 30
Ana | 50
Ben | NULL
Cy | 20', '{}'::text[], array['Ana | 30
Ana | 50
Ben | NULL
Cy | 20', 'Ana | 30
Ana | 50
Cy | 20', 'Ana | 30
Ana | 50
Ben | 0
Cy | 20', 'Ana | 80
Ben | NULL
Cy | 20']::text[], 'SELECT c.name, o.total_amount
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
ORDER BY c.name, o.total_amount', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[[1,"Ana"],[2,"Ben"],[3,"Cy"]]},{"name":"orders","columns":["id","customer_id","total_amount"],"rows":[[10,1,50],[11,1,30],[12,3,20]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A LEFT JOIN keeps every customer: Ana matches two orders (two rows), Cy one, and Ben none, so Ben appears once with NULL, not 0. Dropping Ben would be inner-join behaviour, and nothing is summed without GROUP BY.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(219, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Joins', 'Find suppliers that have no products at all, using the LEFT JOIN anti-join pattern.', 'SELECT s.name FROM suppliers s LEFT JOIN products p ON s.id = p.supplier_id WHERE p.id IS NULL', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT s.name', 'FROM suppliers s', 'LEFT JOIN products p', 'ON s.id = p.supplier_id', 'WHERE p.id IS NULL']::text[], array['JOIN products p', 'WHERE p.id = NULL']::text[], null, 'The LEFT JOIN keeps every supplier, and suppliers with no products get NULL in all p columns; WHERE p.id IS NULL keeps just those. An inner JOIN would drop them before the filter, and = NULL is never true.', array['products', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(220, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'UNION', 'List every city from customers followed by every city from employees, keeping duplicates (we''ll count them later). One column: city.', 'SELECT city FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT city FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Skip the de-duplication step.', 'UNION ALL simply appends the second result to the first without removing duplicates, which also saves the cost of de-duplication. Use it whenever duplicates are wanted or impossible.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(221, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'UNION', 'In this query, what does ORDER BY name sort?', 'The whole combined result', '{}'::text[], array['The whole combined result', 'Only the suppliers rows', 'Only the customers rows', 'Nothing: it is a syntax error']::text[], 'SELECT name FROM customers
UNION
SELECT name FROM suppliers
ORDER BY name', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'An ORDER BY after the last SELECT applies to the entire result of the set operation. Sorting a single branch would need parentheses around it, and the final order still isn''t guaranteed without an outer ORDER BY.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(222, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'UNION', 'This should list the names and cities of all customers and employees, but it errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, city FROM customers UNION SELECT name, city FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, city FROM customers UNION SELECT name FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Count the columns on each side.', 'Every SELECT in a UNION must return the same number of columns, matched by position. Adding city to the second SELECT makes both return (name, city).', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(223, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'CASE', 'Map order status to a state column with a simple CASE on status: ''pending'' → ''open'', ''cancelled'' → ''void'', anything else → ''closed''. Return id, state.', 'SELECT id, CASE status WHEN ''pending'' THEN ''open'' WHEN ''cancelled'' THEN ''void'' ELSE ''closed'' END AS state FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Simple CASE: name the column once, then list the values.', 'A simple CASE compares one expression with each WHEN value using =. It is shorthand for CASE WHEN status = ''pending'' THEN … and suits straight value-to-value mappings.', array['orders']::text[]),
(224, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'CASE', 'What does this CASE return for an employee earning 120000?', 'mid', '{}'::text[], array['mid', 'high', 'low', 'NULL']::text[], 'CASE
  WHEN salary > 50000 THEN ''mid''
  WHEN salary > 100000 THEN ''high''
  ELSE ''low''
END', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'CASE stops at the first true WHEN. 120000 > 50000 is already true, so ''mid'' is returned and the ''high'' branch can never be reached; put the most specific condition first.', '{}'::text[]),
(225, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Joins', 'For every order line, show when it was ordered and what was bought. Join order_items oi to orders o, then to products p (inner joins, in that order). Return o.order_date, p.name, oi.quantity.', 'SELECT o.order_date, p.name, oi.quantity FROM order_items oi JOIN orders o ON oi.order_id = o.id JOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.id', array['SELECT o.order_date, p.name, oi.quantity FROM order_items oi JOIN orders o ON o.id = oi.order_id JOIN products p ON p.id = oi.product_id', 'SELECT o.order_date, p.name, oi.quantity FROM order_items oi JOIN orders o ON o.id = oi.order_id JOIN products p ON oi.product_id = p.id', 'SELECT o.order_date, p.name, oi.quantity FROM order_items oi JOIN orders o ON oi.order_id = o.id JOIN products p ON p.id = oi.product_id']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'order_items is the bridge: it holds both order_id and product_id.', 'order_items is a junction table, so you chain joins through it: each JOIN adds one table with its own ON condition. Every later ON can reference any alias introduced before it.', array['orders', 'products', 'order_items']::text[]),
(226, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Subqueries', 'Ana has two orders. What does this return?', 'Ana
Cy', '{}'::text[], array['Ana
Cy', 'Ana
Ana
Cy', 'Ben', 'Ana
Ben
Cy']::text[], 'SELECT name
FROM customers
WHERE id IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders)
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[[1,"Ana"],[2,"Ben"],[3,"Cy"]]},{"name":"orders","columns":["id","customer_id"],"rows":[[10,1],[11,1],[12,3]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'IN only asks whether a match exists, so Ana appears once even though she has two orders. Joining customers to orders instead would return Ana twice.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(227, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Window Functions', 'Salaries 100, 90, 90, 80 are ranked with ORDER BY salary DESC. What do RANK() and DENSE_RANK() give the 80?', 'RANK 4, DENSE_RANK 3', '{}'::text[], array['RANK 4, DENSE_RANK 3', 'RANK 3, DENSE_RANK 4', 'Both give 4', 'Both give 3']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Both give the tied 90s rank 2. RANK then skips ahead (next is 4), while DENSE_RANK leaves no gaps (next is 3). ROW_NUMBER would number them 1 to 4, breaking the tie arbitrarily.', '{}'::text[]),
(228, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'UNION', 'This should list all customer and supplier names, de-duplicated and alphabetical. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name FROM customers UNION SELECT name FROM suppliers ORDER BY name', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name FROM customers ORDER BY name UNION SELECT name FROM suppliers', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Where may ORDER BY appear in a set operation?', 'ORDER BY sorts the whole UNION result, so it must come after the last SELECT. Inside an unparenthesised branch it is a syntax error.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(229, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Window Functions', 'Number each user''s purchase events in time order as purchase_no, using a running COUNT and considering only ''purchase'' events.', 'SELECT user_id, event_time, COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time) AS purchase_no FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''purchase'' ORDER BY user_id, event_time', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT user_id, event_time,', 'COUNT(*) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time) AS purchase_no', 'FROM user_events', 'WHERE event_type = ''purchase''', 'ORDER BY user_id, event_time']::text[], array['GROUP BY user_id']::text[], null, 'WHERE runs before window functions, so only purchase rows are numbered. COUNT(*) with ORDER BY inside OVER counts rows from the start of the partition up to the current one, restarting for each user.', array['user_events']::text[]),
(230, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Subqueries', 'List products that have never been ordered. Alias products p and use NOT EXISTS with a correlated SELECT 1 FROM order_items oi. Return p.name.', 'SELECT p.name FROM products p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items oi WHERE oi.product_id = p.id)', array['SELECT p.name FROM products p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items oi WHERE p.id = oi.product_id)', 'SELECT p.name FROM products p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT * FROM order_items oi WHERE oi.product_id = p.id)', 'SELECT name FROM products p WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM order_items oi WHERE oi.product_id = p.id)']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The inner query must refer back to the outer product.', 'NOT EXISTS is true when the correlated subquery finds no row for the current product. Unlike NOT IN, it stays correct when the subquery''s column contains NULLs.', array['products', 'order_items']::text[]),
(231, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Joins', 'Why does this query drop customers who have no orders, even though it uses a LEFT JOIN?', 'o.status is NULL for them, so the WHERE test fails', '{}'::text[], array['o.status is NULL for them, so the WHERE test fails', 'A LEFT JOIN never keeps unmatched rows if there is a WHERE', 'WHERE is applied before the join, so they never get joined', 'Text must be compared with LIKE inside an outer join']::text[], 'SELECT c.name, o.id
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id
WHERE o.status = ''shipped''', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'WHERE runs after the join, and for unmatched customers o.status is NULL; NULL = ''shipped'' is not true, so those rows are removed and the query behaves like an inner join. Move the condition into the ON clause to keep them.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(232, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Subqueries', 'This returns no rows, although some customers have never ordered. What is the most likely cause?', 'orders.customer_id contains at least one NULL', '{}'::text[], array['orders.customer_id contains at least one NULL', 'NOT IN cannot be used with a subquery', 'The subquery returns duplicate customer ids', 'NOT IN needs a GROUP BY inside the subquery']::text[], 'SELECT name FROM customers
WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT customer_id FROM orders)', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'x NOT IN (…, NULL) can never be true: when x matches none of the values, the comparison with NULL is unknown, so the whole test is NULL and every row is filtered out. Use NOT EXISTS, or exclude NULLs in the subquery.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(233, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'UNION', 'Both tables contain a NULL country. What does UNION return?', 'Italy
Spain
NULL', '{}'::text[], array['Italy
Spain
NULL', 'Italy
Spain
NULL
NULL', 'Italy
Spain', 'NULL
Italy
Spain']::text[], 'SELECT country FROM customers
UNION
SELECT country FROM suppliers
ORDER BY country', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["country"],"rows":[["Spain"],[null]]},{"name":"suppliers","columns":["country"],"rows":[[null],["Italy"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'When removing duplicates, set operations treat NULLs as equal, so the two NULLs collapse into one row (even though NULL = NULL is not true). PostgreSQL sorts NULLs last in ascending order.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(234, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'UNION', 'Which cities have both customers and employees? Use a set operator (not a join), querying customers first. Return city.', 'SELECT city FROM customers INTERSECT SELECT city FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'You want the overlap of two result sets.', 'INTERSECT returns the distinct rows that appear in both results. Rows are compared as whole tuples, and unlike =, set operations treat two NULLs as equal.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(235, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'CASE', 'This should show each product''s availability: ''sold out'' when stock is 0, otherwise the stock number as text. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN ''sold out'' ELSE stock::text END AS availability FROM products', array['SELECT name, CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN ''sold out'' ELSE CAST(stock AS text) END AS availability FROM products']::text[], null, 'SELECT name, CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN ''sold out'' ELSE stock END AS availability FROM products', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'All branches of a CASE must share one result type.', 'Every CASE branch must resolve to a common type. With an integer ELSE, PostgreSQL tries to read ''sold out'' as an integer and fails; casting stock to text makes both branches text.', array['products']::text[]),
(236, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'UNION', 'Count people per city across customers and employees combined (every person counts), busiest city first.', 'SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS people FROM (SELECT city FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT city FROM employees) t GROUP BY city ORDER BY people DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT city, COUNT(*) AS people', 'FROM (SELECT city FROM customers', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT city FROM employees) t', 'GROUP BY city', 'ORDER BY people DESC']::text[], array['UNION']::text[], null, 'UNION ALL stacks both city lists without removing duplicates, so every person is counted; UNION would collapse repeated cities first. The combined rows live in a derived table that the outer query groups.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(237, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'UNION', 'Where does the result of a UNION get its column names?', 'From the first SELECT', '{}'::text[], array['From the first SELECT', 'From the last SELECT', 'Every SELECT must use the same names', 'They are auto-named column1, column2, …']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The first SELECT''s column names and aliases name the result; later SELECTs only need the same number of columns with compatible types. That''s why an ORDER BY on a UNION uses the first SELECT''s names.', '{}'::text[]),
(238, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'CASE', 'Per department, count employees earning over 100000 as high_earners, using SUM over a CASE that yields 1 or 0. Return department, high_earners.', 'SELECT department, SUM(CASE WHEN salary > 100000 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS high_earners FROM employees GROUP BY department', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Turn the condition into 1 or 0, then add it up per group.', 'A CASE inside an aggregate makes a conditional count: each row contributes 1 or 0 and SUM adds them per department. Unlike WHERE salary > 100000, departments with no high earners still appear, with 0.', array['employees']::text[]),
(239, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'CASE', 'Which CASE form lets each WHEN branch test a different column?', 'Searched CASE: CASE WHEN cond THEN …', '{}'::text[], array['Searched CASE: CASE WHEN cond THEN …', 'Simple CASE: CASE col WHEN value THEN …', 'Both forms can', 'Neither: you need nested CASEs']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A simple CASE compares one expression with a list of values using =. A searched CASE takes a full boolean condition in each WHEN, so branches can test different columns, ranges or IS NULL.', '{}'::text[]),
(240, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'CASE', 'Bob''s bonus is NULL. What does this return?', 'Ann | yes
Bob | no
Cy | no', '{}'::text[], array['Ann | yes
Bob | no
Cy | no', 'Ann | yes
Bob | NULL
Cy | no', 'Ann | yes
Cy | no', 'Ann | yes
Bob | yes
Cy | no']::text[], 'SELECT name,
       CASE WHEN bonus > 0 THEN ''yes'' ELSE ''no'' END AS has_bonus
FROM employees
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["name","bonus"],"rows":[["Ann",500],["Bob",null],["Cy",0]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'For Bob, NULL > 0 is unknown, and CASE only takes a branch whose condition is true, so he falls through to ELSE ''no''. A CASE returns NULL only when nothing matches and there is no ELSE.', array['employees']::text[]),
(241, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Window Functions', 'This should show a running total of each customer''s spending over time. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT customer_id, order_date, SUM(total_amount) OVER (PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date) AS running_total FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT customer_id, order_date, SUM(total_amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date PARTITION BY customer_id) AS running_total FROM orders', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Inside OVER, clause order matters.', 'Inside OVER the order is fixed: PARTITION BY, then ORDER BY, then an optional frame clause. The partition restarts the running total for each customer.', array['orders']::text[]),
(242, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Subqueries', 'Find customers with at least one ''delivered'' order, using a correlated EXISTS.', 'SELECT c.name FROM customers c WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id AND o.status = ''delivered'')', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT c.name', 'FROM customers c', 'WHERE EXISTS', '(SELECT 1 FROM orders o', 'WHERE o.customer_id = c.id AND o.status = ''delivered'')']::text[], array['WHERE c.id IN']::text[], null, 'EXISTS is true when the correlated subquery finds at least one row for the current customer. The subquery''s WHERE ties it to the outer row (o.customer_id = c.id) and applies the delivered filter.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(243, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Window Functions', 'Why does WHERE ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) = 1 raise an error?', 'Window functions are evaluated after WHERE', '{}'::text[], array['Window functions are evaluated after WHERE', 'ROW_NUMBER requires a PARTITION BY clause', 'The condition belongs in HAVING, not WHERE', 'ROW_NUMBER starts counting at 0, not 1']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Window functions are computed after FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY and HAVING, so they aren''t allowed in WHERE. Compute the number in a subquery or CTE, then filter on it outside.', '{}'::text[]),
(244, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Window Functions', 'Number employees within each department from highest to lowest salary, as rn, using ROW_NUMBER. Return name, department, rn.', 'SELECT name, department, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'PARTITION BY restarts the numbering; ORDER BY decides who is first.', 'PARTITION BY splits the rows into departments, and ROW_NUMBER restarts at 1 in each. Unlike GROUP BY, a window function keeps every row.', array['employees']::text[]),
(245, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Joins', 'customers has 8 rows and orders has 10. Every order belongs to an existing customer, and 2 customers have no orders. How many rows does customers LEFT JOIN orders return?', '12', '{}'::text[], array['12', '10', '8', '18']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Each of the 10 orders produces one joined row, and each of the 2 customers without orders adds one NULL-padded row: 10 + 2 = 12. A LEFT JOIN can return more rows than its left table when keys match several times.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(246, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Window Functions', 'What is each employee''s difference from the average?', 'Ann | -10000
Bob | 10000
Cat | -10000
Dan | 10000', '{}'::text[], array['Ann | -10000
Bob | 10000
Cat | -10000
Dan | 10000', 'Ann | -5000
Bob | 15000
Cat | -15000
Dan | 5000', 'Ann | 0
Bob | 0
Cat | 0
Dan | 0', 'HR | 50000
Sales | 60000']::text[], 'SELECT name,
       salary - AVG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department) AS diff
FROM employees
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department","salary"],"rows":[["Ann","Sales",50000],["Bob","Sales",70000],["Cat","HR",40000],["Dan","HR",60000]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The window averages within each department (Sales 60000, HR 50000) and attaches that value to every row, so each salary is compared with its own department''s average. The 55000 company average would need OVER () instead.', array['employees']::text[]),
(247, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Joins', 'This should show order id, order date and customer name for Gold-tier customers, but PostgreSQL rejects it. Fix it.', 'SELECT o.id, order_date, name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE tier = ''Gold''', array['SELECT o.id, o.order_date, c.name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE c.tier = ''Gold''']::text[], null, 'SELECT id, order_date, name FROM orders o JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id WHERE tier = ''Gold''', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Which selected column name exists in both tables?', 'Both orders and customers have an id column, so an unqualified id is ambiguous and PostgreSQL raises an error. Qualify it as o.id; columns that exist in only one table (order_date, name, tier) resolve on their own.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(248, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Subqueries', 'A scalar subquery in the SELECT list returns two rows for some outer row. What does PostgreSQL do?', 'Raises an error: more than one row returned', '{}'::text[], array['Raises an error: more than one row returned', 'Uses the first row the subquery returns', 'Repeats the outer row once per value', 'Returns NULL for that outer row']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A subquery used as an expression may return at most one row. With more, PostgreSQL raises "more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression"; with zero rows the value is NULL.', '{}'::text[]),
(249, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'CASE', 'For each category, count out-of-stock products (stock = 0) as out_of_stock with conditional aggregation, most first.', 'SELECT category, SUM(CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS out_of_stock FROM products GROUP BY category ORDER BY out_of_stock DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT category,', 'SUM(CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS out_of_stock', 'FROM products', 'GROUP BY category', 'ORDER BY out_of_stock DESC']::text[], array['COUNT(CASE WHEN stock = 0 THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS out_of_stock']::text[], null, 'SUM over a 1/0 CASE counts the matching rows in each group. COUNT of the same CASE would count the zeros too, since COUNT only skips NULLs, so it would return every product.', array['products']::text[]),
(250, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Joins', 'Audit time: show every department and every employee, including departments with no staff and employees whose department isn''t in departments. FULL JOIN departments d to employees e on the department name. Return d.name, e.name.', 'SELECT d.name, e.name FROM departments d FULL JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department', array['SELECT d.name, e.name FROM departments d FULL JOIN employees e ON e.department = d.name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'You need the unmatched rows from BOTH sides.', 'FULL [OUTER] JOIN returns the matched pairs plus the unmatched rows of both tables, padding the missing side with NULLs. It combines what a LEFT JOIN and a RIGHT JOIN would each keep.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[])
on conflict (id) do nothing;

insert into public.questions (id, difficulty, type, topic, question, answer, alternatives, options, query, sample_tables, tokens, distractors, hint, explanation, schema_tables) values
(251, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'UNION', 'Why does this query fail?', 'Each column position must have compatible types', '{}'::text[], array['Each column position must have compatible types', 'UNION requires both SELECTs to read the same table', 'Column names must be identical in both SELECTs', 'UNION can''t combine two tables that both have id']::text[], 'SELECT id, name FROM customers
UNION
SELECT name, id FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Set operations match columns by position, not by name. Position 1 mixes integer with text, so PostgreSQL raises "UNION types integer and text cannot be matched".', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(252, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Joins', 'What does this FULL JOIN return?', 'Sales | Ann
Sales | Bob
NULL | Cat
HR | NULL
Legal | NULL', '{}'::text[], array['Sales | Ann
Sales | Bob
NULL | Cat
HR | NULL
Legal | NULL', 'Sales | Ann
Sales | Bob
HR | NULL
Legal | NULL', 'Sales | Ann
Sales | Bob
NULL | Cat', 'Sales | Ann
Sales | Bob']::text[], 'SELECT d.name AS dept, e.name AS emp
FROM departments d
FULL JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department
ORDER BY emp, dept', '[{"name":"departments","columns":["name"],"rows":[["Sales"],["HR"],["Legal"]]},{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department"],"rows":[["Ann","Sales"],["Bob","Sales"],["Cat","IT"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'FULL JOIN keeps the matched pairs plus the unmatched rows of both sides: Cat (department IT) gets a NULL dept, while HR and Legal get a NULL emp. PostgreSQL sorts NULLs last in ascending order, so those rows come at the end.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(253, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'UNION', 'Procurement asks: which countries have customers but no suppliers? Use a set operator, querying customers first. Return country.', 'SELECT country FROM customers EXCEPT SELECT country FROM suppliers', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Start from customer countries and subtract.', 'EXCEPT returns the distinct rows of the first query that don''t appear in the second. Order matters: swapping the two queries would give supplier countries with no customers.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(254, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'CASE', 'Why does COUNT(CASE WHEN status = ''shipped'' THEN 1 END) count only the shipped orders?', 'Other rows give NULL, and COUNT skips NULLs', '{}'::text[], array['Other rows give NULL, and COUNT skips NULLs', 'COUNT only counts cells holding the value 1', 'The CASE filters rows like a WHERE clause', 'It doesn''t: it counts every row']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'With no ELSE, non-matching rows produce NULL, and COUNT(expression) ignores NULLs. Adding ELSE 0 would break it, because 0 is a value and would be counted too.', array['orders']::text[]),
(255, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Subqueries', 'This should return the largest number of products in any single category, but it errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT MAX(product_count) FROM (SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS product_count FROM products GROUP BY category) t', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT MAX(product_count) FROM (SELECT category, COUNT(*) FROM products GROUP BY category) t', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The outer query only sees the column names the derived table exposes.', 'The outer query can only reference columns the derived table exposes. An unaliased COUNT(*) is named count, so product_count doesn''t exist until you alias it inside the subquery.', array['products']::text[]),
(256, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Joins', 'List employees who were hired before their own manager, earliest hire first.', 'SELECT e.name, e.hire_date FROM employees e JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id WHERE e.hire_date < m.hire_date ORDER BY e.hire_date', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT e.name, e.hire_date', 'FROM employees e', 'JOIN employees m', 'ON e.manager_id = m.id', 'WHERE e.hire_date < m.hire_date', 'ORDER BY e.hire_date']::text[], array['ON e.id = m.manager_id']::text[], null, 'In this self join e is the employee and m is their manager, linked by e.manager_id = m.id. Reversing the link (e.id = m.manager_id) would make e the manager and answer a different question.', array['employees']::text[]),
(257, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Window Functions', 'Does the ORDER BY inside OVER (…) decide the order of the query''s output rows?', 'No: it only orders rows for the window', '{}'::text[], array['No: it only orders rows for the window', 'Yes: it replaces the outer ORDER BY', 'Yes, but only within each partition', 'Only when there is no PARTITION BY']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The ORDER BY in OVER defines the sequence the window function sees (for ranking, LAG or running totals). Only an outer ORDER BY guarantees the order of the returned rows.', array['orders']::text[]),
(258, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'CASE', 'List customers Gold first, then Silver, then everyone else, alphabetically within each tier. Sort with a simple CASE on tier (''Gold'' → 1, ''Silver'' → 2, else 3), then name. Return name, tier.', 'SELECT name, tier FROM customers ORDER BY CASE tier WHEN ''Gold'' THEN 1 WHEN ''Silver'' THEN 2 ELSE 3 END, name', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'ORDER BY accepts any expression, not just a column.', 'Sorting by tier alphabetically would give Bronze, Gold, Silver. A CASE in ORDER BY maps each tier to a custom rank, and name then breaks ties within a tier.', array['customers']::text[]),
(259, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Joins', 'Which FROM clause returns the same rows as this RIGHT JOIN?', 'FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id', '{}'::text[], array['FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id', 'FROM orders o LEFT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id', 'FROM customers c RIGHT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id', 'FROM customers c JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id']::text[], 'SELECT c.name, o.id
FROM orders o
RIGHT JOIN customers c ON o.customer_id = c.id', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A RIGHT JOIN keeps every row of the right-hand table (customers). Swapping the table order and using LEFT JOIN is equivalent, which is why many teams standardise on LEFT JOIN.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(260, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Subqueries', 'What does this derived-table query return?', '2', '{}'::text[], array['2', '3', '5', '1']::text[], 'SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM (
  SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS total
  FROM orders
  GROUP BY customer_id
) t
WHERE total > 150', '[{"name":"orders","columns":["customer_id","total_amount"],"rows":[[1,100],[1,300],[2,50],[3,200],[3,200]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The derived table has one row per customer (totals 400, 50 and 400), and the outer WHERE keeps the two above 150. Counting individual orders over 150 would give 3.', array['orders']::text[]),
(261, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'CASE', 'This should give Gold customers a discount of 0.2 and everyone else 0. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, CASE tier WHEN ''Gold'' THEN 0.2 ELSE 0 END AS discount FROM customers', array['SELECT name, CASE WHEN tier = ''Gold'' THEN 0.2 ELSE 0 END AS discount FROM customers']::text[], null, 'SELECT name, CASE tier WHEN tier = ''Gold'' THEN 0.2 ELSE 0 END AS discount FROM customers', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'This mixes up the two CASE syntaxes.', 'In a simple CASE each WHEN holds a value to compare with the CASE expression, so WHEN tier = ''Gold'' compares text with a boolean and errors. Drop "tier =" (simple form) or drop the tier after CASE (searched form).', array['customers']::text[]),
(262, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Subqueries', 'Show departments whose average salary exceeds 70000, highest first, by filtering a derived table of per-department averages.', 'SELECT department, avg_salary FROM (SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary FROM employees GROUP BY department) d WHERE avg_salary > 70000 ORDER BY avg_salary DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT department, avg_salary', 'FROM (SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary', 'FROM employees', 'GROUP BY department) d', 'WHERE avg_salary > 70000', 'ORDER BY avg_salary DESC']::text[], array['HAVING avg_salary > 70000']::text[], null, 'Once the derived table has computed the averages, avg_salary is an ordinary column to the outer query, so a plain WHERE filters it. The outer query has no GROUP BY, so HAVING is not the tool there.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(263, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Subqueries', 'Inside EXISTS (…), what''s the difference between SELECT 1 and SELECT *?', 'None: EXISTS only checks whether any row comes back', '{}'::text[], array['None: EXISTS only checks whether any row comes back', 'SELECT * is slower because it reads every column', 'SELECT 1 is only true if some column holds the value 1', 'SELECT * is not allowed inside EXISTS']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'EXISTS is true as soon as the subquery produces one row; its select list is never evaluated for output. SELECT 1 is just a convention that makes this intent obvious.', '{}'::text[]),
(264, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Window Functions', 'For each event, show when the same user''s next event happened. Return user_id, event_time and next_time, using LEAD partitioned by user_id and ordered by event_time.', 'SELECT user_id, event_time, LEAD(event_time) OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time) AS next_time FROM user_events', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'LEAD looks forward, LAG looks back.', 'LEAD(expr) reads the value from the next row in the window''s order, and the partition keeps it within the same user. Each user''s last event gets NULL because there is no next row.', array['user_events']::text[]),
(265, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Window Functions', 'What does LAG(salary, 1, 0) OVER (ORDER BY hire_date) return for the earliest-hired employee?', '0', '{}'::text[], array['0', 'NULL', 'Their own salary', 'The latest hire''s salary']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LAG''s third argument is the default used when the offset row doesn''t exist. The first row has no previous row, so it gets 0 instead of the usual NULL.', '{}'::text[]),
(266, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'UNION', 'What does INTERSECT return for these two-column rows?', 'Ana | Paris', '{}'::text[], array['Ana | Paris', 'Ana | Paris
Ben | Rome', 'Ana | Paris
Ben | Oslo
Ben | Rome', '(no rows)']::text[], 'SELECT name, city FROM customers
INTERSECT
SELECT name, city FROM employees
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["name","city"],"rows":[["Ana","Paris"],["Ben","Rome"],["Cy","Oslo"]]},{"name":"employees","columns":["name","city"],"rows":[["Ana","Paris"],["Ben","Oslo"],["Dee","Rome"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'INTERSECT compares entire rows: Ben appears in both tables, but (Ben, Rome) and (Ben, Oslo) are different rows. Only (Ana, Paris) exists in both results.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(267, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Window Functions', 'This should show how each employee''s salary differs from the previous hire''s in the same department (salary_diff). It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT name, hire_date, salary - LAG(salary) OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY hire_date) AS salary_diff FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, hire_date, salary - LAG(salary) OVER (GROUP BY department ORDER BY hire_date) AS salary_diff FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Windows are split into groups with a different keyword.', 'OVER uses PARTITION BY, not GROUP BY, to split rows into groups; GROUP BY there is a syntax error. The first hire in each department gets NULL, since LAG has no earlier row.', array['employees']::text[]),
(268, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Joins', 'Count orders per customer as order_count. Customers with no orders must show 0. LEFT JOIN customers c to orders o and group by c.name. Return c.name, order_count.', 'SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.name', array['SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.customer_id = c.id GROUP BY c.name', 'SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.customer_id) AS order_count FROM customers c LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.id = o.customer_id GROUP BY c.name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'COUNT(*) would count the NULL-padded row as 1.', 'COUNT(o.id) skips NULLs, so a customer whose only joined row is NULL-padded gets 0. COUNT(*) counts rows, so it would wrongly report 1 order for those customers.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(269, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Window Functions', 'Show a 3-order moving average of total_amount (the current order and the two before it by order_date) as moving_avg.', 'SELECT order_date, total_amount, AVG(total_amount) OVER ( ORDER BY order_date ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW ) AS moving_avg FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT order_date, total_amount,', 'AVG(total_amount) OVER (', 'ORDER BY order_date', 'ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', ') AS moving_avg', 'FROM orders']::text[], array['PARTITION BY order_date']::text[], null, 'A frame clause narrows the window: ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW averages the current row and the two before it. The frame always comes after ORDER BY inside OVER.', array['orders']::text[]),
(270, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Joins', 'What does this self join return?', 'Employees who earn more than their own manager', '{}'::text[], array['Employees who earn more than their own manager', 'Managers who earn more than their direct reports', 'Employees who earn more than every manager', 'Managers who have at least one direct report']::text[], 'SELECT e.name
FROM employees e
JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id
WHERE e.salary > m.salary', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The aliases give the table two roles: e is the employee and m is the row that e.manager_id points to. The WHERE compares each employee with their own manager, and SELECT e.name returns the employee.', array['employees']::text[]),
(271, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Subqueries', 'What does this query return?', 'The second-highest distinct salary', '{}'::text[], array['The second-highest distinct salary', 'The highest salary', 'The lowest salary', 'Every salary below the highest']::text[], 'SELECT MAX(salary)
FROM employees
WHERE salary < (SELECT MAX(salary) FROM employees)', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The subquery finds the top salary, WHERE removes every row at that value, and the outer MAX picks the largest one left. Ties at the top are removed together, so you get the second-highest distinct value.', array['employees']::text[]),
(272, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Window Functions', 'Show each product''s name, price and its category''s average price as category_avg, keeping one row per product (no GROUP BY).', 'SELECT name, price, AVG(price) OVER (PARTITION BY category) AS category_avg FROM products', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'An aggregate becomes a window function when you add OVER.', 'AVG(...) OVER (PARTITION BY category) computes the category average and attaches it to every row, so each product can be compared with its group. Without ORDER BY in OVER, the frame is the whole partition.', array['products']::text[]),
(273, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'CASE', 'How many products land in each band?', 'cheap | 1
mid | 2
pricey | 2', '{}'::text[], array['cheap | 1
mid | 2
pricey | 2', 'cheap | 2
mid | 2
pricey | 1', 'cheap | 1
mid | 3
pricey | 2', 'cheap | 1
mid | 3
pricey | 5']::text[], 'SELECT CASE WHEN price < 20 THEN ''cheap''
            WHEN price < 100 THEN ''mid''
            ELSE ''pricey'' END AS band,
       COUNT(*) AS n
FROM products
GROUP BY band
ORDER BY band', '[{"name":"products","columns":["price"],"rows":[[5],[20],[25],[100],[300]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Each row lands in exactly one band: the first WHEN that is true. 20 fails price < 20 and becomes ''mid'', and 100 fails price < 100 and becomes ''pricey''. PostgreSQL also lets GROUP BY use the output alias band.', array['products']::text[]),
(274, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Joins', 'This should list departments that have no employees at all. Fix both bugs.', 'SELECT d.name FROM departments d LEFT JOIN employees e ON d.name = e.department WHERE e.id IS NULL', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT d.name FROM departments d LEFT JION employees e ON d.name = e.department WHERE e.id = NULL', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One typo, and one comparison that can never be true.', 'JION is a typo for JOIN. And e.id = NULL is never true (it yields NULL), so the anti-join would return nothing; IS NULL keeps the departments whose LEFT JOIN found no employee.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(275, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'Joins', 'This query leaves out the CEO, whose manager_id is NULL. Which change keeps the CEO, with a NULL manager?', 'Change JOIN to LEFT JOIN', '{}'::text[], array['Change JOIN to LEFT JOIN', 'Change JOIN to RIGHT JOIN', 'Add WHERE e.manager_id IS NULL', 'Rewrite the ON as m.id = e.manager_id']::text[], 'SELECT e.name, m.name AS manager
FROM employees e
JOIN employees m ON e.manager_id = m.id', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'An inner join drops e rows with no match, and NULL never equals any id. A LEFT JOIN keeps every e row and fills m.name with NULL for the CEO; swapping the sides of = changes nothing.', array['employees']::text[]),
(276, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'UNION', 'Find customer ids that have a ''delivered'' order but no ''cancelled'' order, in id order.', 'SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''delivered'' EXCEPT SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''cancelled'' ORDER BY customer_id', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''delivered''', 'EXCEPT', 'SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''cancelled''', 'ORDER BY customer_id']::text[], array['INTERSECT', 'UNION ALL']::text[], null, 'EXCEPT keeps the distinct rows of the first query that are missing from the second, so the delivered set must come first. INTERSECT would instead return customers who have both kinds of order.', array['orders']::text[]),
(277, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Joins', 'The status filter sits in the ON clause. What is the result?', 'Ana | 1
Ben | 0
Cy | 2', '{}'::text[], array['Ana | 1
Ben | 0
Cy | 2', 'Ana | 1
Cy | 2', 'Ana | 1
Ben | 1
Cy | 2', 'Ana | 2
Ben | 1
Cy | 2']::text[], 'SELECT c.name, COUNT(o.id) AS shipped
FROM customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o
  ON c.id = o.customer_id AND o.status = ''shipped''
GROUP BY c.name
ORDER BY c.name', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[[1,"Ana"],[2,"Ben"],[3,"Cy"]]},{"name":"orders","columns":["id","customer_id","status"],"rows":[[10,1,"shipped"],[11,1,"pending"],[12,2,"pending"],[13,3,"shipped"],[14,3,"shipped"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A condition in ON only limits which orders match; Ben is still kept with a NULL-padded row, and COUNT(o.id) counts that as 0. The same test in WHERE would remove Ben entirely.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(278, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Subqueries', 'Find employees who earn more than the average salary of their own department. Alias the outer table e and the inner one e2. Return e.name, e.department.', 'SELECT e.name, e.department FROM employees e WHERE e.salary > (SELECT AVG(e2.salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.department = e.department)', array['SELECT e.name, e.department FROM employees e WHERE e.salary > (SELECT AVG(e2.salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e.department = e2.department)', 'SELECT e.name, e.department FROM employees e WHERE e.salary > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees e2 WHERE e2.department = e.department)']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The inner AVG must be limited to the outer row''s department.', 'The subquery is correlated: it references e.department, so it is evaluated for each outer employee and averages only that department. Without the correlation you would compare against the company-wide average.', array['employees']::text[]),
(279, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Joins', 'Finance wants total revenue (quantity × unit_price) per supplier, highest first. Put the pieces in order.', 'SELECT s.name, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue FROM suppliers s JOIN products p ON s.id = p.supplier_id JOIN order_items oi ON p.id = oi.product_id GROUP BY s.name ORDER BY revenue DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT s.name, SUM(oi.quantity * oi.unit_price) AS revenue', 'FROM suppliers s', 'JOIN products p ON s.id = p.supplier_id', 'JOIN order_items oi ON p.id = oi.product_id', 'GROUP BY s.name', 'ORDER BY revenue DESC']::text[], array['GROUP BY p.name', 'WHERE revenue > 0']::text[], null, 'Suppliers reach order lines through products, so the joins chain suppliers → products → order_items, and each ON may only use aliases already introduced. A SELECT alias like revenue works in ORDER BY but not in WHERE.', array['products', 'order_items', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(280, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Subqueries', 'Who earns more than their department''s average?', 'Bob
Eve', '{}'::text[], array['Bob
Eve', 'Ann
Bob
Eve', 'Bob
Dan
Eve', 'Eve']::text[], 'SELECT name
FROM employees e
WHERE salary > (
  SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees
  WHERE department = e.department
)
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department","salary"],"rows":[["Ann","Sales",50000],["Bob","Sales",70000],["Cat","HR",30000],["Dan","HR",40000],["Eve","HR",50000]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The subquery is correlated on department, so Sales rows are compared with 60000 and HR rows with 40000. Ann''s 50000 beats the company average (48000) but not her department''s, and Dan only equals his, which isn''t greater.', array['employees']::text[]),
(281, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'UNION', 'Table a holds v = 1, 1, 2, 3 and table b holds v = 2, 3, 3, 4. How many rows does this return?', '1', '{}'::text[], array['1', '2', '0', '3']::text[], 'SELECT v FROM a
EXCEPT
SELECT v FROM b', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'EXCEPT removes duplicates, returning the distinct values of a that are absent from b: just 1, once. EXCEPT ALL would keep both copies of 1 and return 2 rows.', '{}'::text[]),
(282, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'UNION', 'Ana is both a customer (twice) and a supplier. What does this return?', 'Ana | customer
Ana | supplier
Ben | customer
Cy | supplier', '{}'::text[], array['Ana | customer
Ana | supplier
Ben | customer
Cy | supplier', 'Ana | customer
Ana | customer
Ana | supplier
Ben | customer
Cy | supplier', 'Ana | customer
Ben | customer
Cy | supplier', 'Ana | supplier
Ben | customer
Cy | supplier']::text[], 'SELECT name, ''customer'' AS kind FROM customers
UNION
SELECT name, ''supplier'' FROM suppliers
ORDER BY name, kind', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["name"],"rows":[["Ana"],["Ana"],["Ben"]]},{"name":"suppliers","columns":["name"],"rows":[["Ana"],["Cy"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'UNION de-duplicates whole rows, including duplicates within one branch, so the two (Ana, customer) rows collapse. (Ana, supplier) differs in the kind column, so it survives as a separate row.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(283, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'Subqueries', 'This should list departments whose average salary is above the company-wide average. Fix it.', 'SELECT department, AVG(salary) FROM employees GROUP BY department HAVING AVG(salary) > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees)', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT department, AVG(salary) FROM employees WHERE AVG(salary) > (SELECT AVG(salary) FROM employees) GROUP BY department', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Filtering on a group''s aggregate needs a different clause, in a different spot.', 'WHERE filters individual rows before grouping, so it can''t use AVG(). Group-level conditions go in HAVING, which comes after GROUP BY and can compare against a scalar subquery.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(284, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'UNION', 'Build a contact directory: name plus a column called source holding ''customer'' or ''employee''. UNION ALL customers then employees, sorted by name.', 'SELECT name, ''customer'' AS source FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT name, ''employee'' FROM employees ORDER BY name', array['SELECT name, ''customer'' AS source FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT name, ''employee'' AS source FROM employees ORDER BY name']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Label each branch with a literal; one ORDER BY goes at the very end.', 'Result column names come from the first SELECT, so aliasing source there is enough. A single ORDER BY after the last SELECT sorts the whole combined result.', array['employees', 'customers']::text[]),
(285, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Subqueries', 'List employees who manage nobody, using NOT IN. Make it NULL-safe: the CEO''s manager_id is NULL.', 'SELECT name FROM employees WHERE id NOT IN (SELECT manager_id FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NOT NULL)', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name', 'FROM employees', 'WHERE id NOT IN', '(SELECT manager_id FROM employees', 'WHERE manager_id IS NOT NULL)']::text[], array['WHERE manager_id <> NULL)']::text[], null, 'The CEO''s NULL manager_id would make every NOT IN test NULL and return no rows, so the subquery filters it out with IS NOT NULL. <> NULL is never true, so it would empty the subquery and return everyone.', array['employees']::text[]),
(286, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'CASE', 'What do these two averages return?', '50000 | 25000', '{}'::text[], array['50000 | 25000', '50000 | 50000', '25000 | 25000', '65000 | 25000']::text[], 'SELECT AVG(CASE WHEN department = ''Sales'' THEN salary END) AS a,
       AVG(CASE WHEN department = ''Sales'' THEN salary ELSE 0 END) AS b
FROM employees', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["department","salary"],"rows":[["Sales",40000],["Sales",60000],["HR",70000],["HR",90000]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Without ELSE, non-Sales rows become NULL and AVG ignores them, giving the Sales average. With ELSE 0 those rows count as zeros, dragging the average down to (40000 + 60000) / 4.', array['employees']::text[]),
(287, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Window Functions', 'Two orders share a date. What running totals come out?', '2024-01-01 | 100 | 100
2024-01-02 | 50 | 175
2024-01-02 | 25 | 175
2024-01-03 | 10 | 185', '{}'::text[], array['2024-01-01 | 100 | 100
2024-01-02 | 50 | 175
2024-01-02 | 25 | 175
2024-01-03 | 10 | 185', '2024-01-01 | 100 | 100
2024-01-02 | 50 | 150
2024-01-02 | 25 | 175
2024-01-03 | 10 | 185', '2024-01-01 | 100 | 185
2024-01-02 | 50 | 185
2024-01-02 | 25 | 185
2024-01-03 | 10 | 185', '2024-01-01 | 100 | 100
2024-01-02 | 50 | 75
2024-01-02 | 25 | 75
2024-01-03 | 10 | 10']::text[], 'SELECT order_date, total_amount,
       SUM(total_amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date) AS running
FROM orders
ORDER BY order_date, total_amount DESC', '[{"name":"orders","columns":["order_date","total_amount"],"rows":[["2024-01-01",100],["2024-01-02",50],["2024-01-02",25],["2024-01-03",10]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'With ORDER BY and no frame clause, the default frame is RANGE UNBOUNDED PRECEDING to CURRENT ROW, which includes every row tied on order_date. Both 2024-01-02 rows see 100 + 50 + 25 = 175; ROWS BETWEEN would accumulate strictly row by row.', array['orders']::text[]),
(288, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'CASE', 'In one row, return delivered_revenue (total_amount of ''delivered'' orders, via SUM of a CASE with ELSE 0) and total_revenue (SUM of all total_amount), in that order.', 'SELECT SUM(CASE WHEN status = ''delivered'' THEN total_amount ELSE 0 END) AS delivered_revenue, SUM(total_amount) AS total_revenue FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One pass over orders; the CASE decides what each row contributes.', 'Conditional aggregation computes several differently filtered totals in a single scan. A WHERE status = ''delivered'' would also shrink total_revenue, which is why the condition lives inside the CASE.', array['orders']::text[]),
(289, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'UNION', 'One alphabetical list of German customers and German suppliers, with a kind column labelling each row ''customer'' or ''supplier''.', 'SELECT name, ''customer'' AS kind FROM customers WHERE country = ''Germany'' UNION ALL SELECT name, ''supplier'' FROM suppliers WHERE country = ''Germany'' ORDER BY name', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name, ''customer'' AS kind', 'FROM customers', 'WHERE country = ''Germany''', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT name, ''supplier''', 'FROM suppliers', 'WHERE country = ''Germany''', 'ORDER BY name']::text[], array['HAVING country = ''Germany''']::text[], null, 'Each branch of a set operation has its own FROM and WHERE, and the result''s column names (kind) come from the first SELECT. The single ORDER BY at the end sorts the combined list.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(290, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Joins', 'How many direct reports does each person have?', 'Ava | 2
Ben | 1
Cal | 0
Dee | 0', '{}'::text[], array['Ava | 2
Ben | 1
Cal | 0
Dee | 0', 'Ava | 2
Ben | 1', 'Ava | 2
Ben | 1
Cal | 1
Dee | 1', 'Ben | 1
Cal | 1
Dee | 1
Ava | 0']::text[], 'SELECT m.name, COUNT(e.id) AS reports
FROM employees m
LEFT JOIN employees e ON e.manager_id = m.id
GROUP BY m.name
ORDER BY reports DESC, m.name', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["id","name","manager_id"],"rows":[[1,"Ava",null],[2,"Ben",1],[3,"Cal",1],[4,"Dee",2]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Here m plays the manager and e the report, and the LEFT JOIN keeps every m row. COUNT(e.id) skips the NULL-padded rows, so people with no reports show 0; COUNT(*) would give them 1.', array['employees']::text[]),
(291, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Subqueries', 'One order has a NULL customer_id. What does NOT EXISTS return?', 'Ben', '{}'::text[], array['Ben', '(no rows)', 'Ana
Cy', 'Ana
Ben
Cy']::text[], 'SELECT name
FROM customers c
WHERE NOT EXISTS (
  SELECT 1 FROM orders o WHERE o.customer_id = c.id
)
ORDER BY name', '[{"name":"customers","columns":["id","name"],"rows":[[1,"Ana"],[2,"Ben"],[3,"Cy"]]},{"name":"orders","columns":["id","customer_id"],"rows":[[10,1],[11,null],[12,3]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'NOT EXISTS asks "is there any matching order row?". The NULL customer_id never equals an id, so it simply matches nothing and Ben (no orders) is returned. NOT IN on the same data would return no rows because of the NULL.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(292, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Window Functions', 'Top 3 earners per department, ties included. In a derived table t, add RANK() by salary (highest first) per department as rnk, then keep rnk <= 3. Return name, department, salary.', 'SELECT name, department, salary FROM (SELECT name, department, salary, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees) t WHERE rnk <= 3', array['SELECT name, department, salary FROM (SELECT name, department, salary, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees) t WHERE rnk < 4']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Window results can''t be filtered in the same query''s WHERE.', 'Window functions are evaluated after WHERE, so you rank inside a derived table and filter in the outer query. RANK gives tied salaries the same number, so a tie for 3rd place keeps both rows.', array['employees']::text[]),
(293, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'Window Functions', 'Show products at the top two price levels in each category (tied prices share a level, no gaps), using DENSE_RANK in a derived table.', 'SELECT name, category, price FROM (SELECT name, category, price, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS dr FROM products) t WHERE dr <= 2', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name, category, price', 'FROM (SELECT name, category, price,', 'DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS dr', 'FROM products) t', 'WHERE dr <= 2']::text[], array['HAVING dr <= 2']::text[], null, 'The rank is computed in a derived table because window functions can''t be used in WHERE. DENSE_RANK gives tied prices the same level without gaps, so dr <= 2 keeps the two highest distinct prices per category.', array['products']::text[]),
(294, 'hard', 'multiple-choice', 'CASE', 'Why does this label every employee ''Staff'', even the CEO whose manager_id is NULL?', 'manager_id = NULL is never true', '{}'::text[], array['manager_id = NULL is never true', 'The parser ignores WHEN NULL', 'manager_id must be cast to text', 'ELSE overrides earlier WHENs']::text[], 'CASE manager_id WHEN NULL THEN ''CEO'' ELSE ''Staff'' END', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A simple CASE compares with =, and manager_id = NULL yields NULL rather than true, so the branch never fires. Use a searched CASE instead: CASE WHEN manager_id IS NULL THEN ''CEO'' ELSE ''Staff'' END.', '{}'::text[]),
(295, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'CASE', 'What does this conditional aggregation return?', '1 | 1 | 2
2 | 2 | 2
3 | 0 | 1', '{}'::text[], array['1 | 1 | 2
2 | 2 | 2
3 | 0 | 1', '1 | 1 | 1
2 | 2 | 2', '1 | 1 | 2
2 | 2 | 2
3 | NULL | 1', '1 | 2 | 2
2 | 2 | 2
3 | 1 | 1']::text[], 'SELECT customer_id,
       SUM(CASE WHEN status = ''shipped'' THEN 1 ELSE 0 END) AS shipped,
       COUNT(*) AS total
FROM orders
GROUP BY customer_id
ORDER BY customer_id', '[{"name":"orders","columns":["customer_id","status"],"rows":[[1,"shipped"],[1,"cancelled"],[2,"shipped"],[2,"shipped"],[3,"pending"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Every row goes through the CASE, adding 1 if shipped and 0 otherwise, so customer 3 gets 0 instead of disappearing (as a WHERE filter would cause). ELSE 0 also makes the sum 0 rather than NULL.', array['orders']::text[]),
(296, 'hard', 'fix-query', 'UNION', 'This should return two rows, a label and a row count, for customers and for suppliers. It errors. Fix it.', 'SELECT ''customers'' AS source, COUNT(*) AS n FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT ''suppliers'', COUNT(*) FROM suppliers', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT ''customers'' AS source, COUNT(*) AS n FROM customers UNION ALL SELECT COUNT(*), ''suppliers'' FROM suppliers', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Columns are paired by position, not by name.', 'Set operations pair columns by position, so the second SELECT must also return the label first and the count second. Otherwise PostgreSQL tries to read ''customers'' as a number and fails.', array['customers', 'suppliers']::text[]),
(297, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Window Functions', 'How many days passed since each customer''s previous order?', '1 | 2024-01-05 | NULL
1 | 2024-01-20 | 15
1 | 2024-02-01 | 12
2 | 2024-01-07 | NULL', '{}'::text[], array['1 | 2024-01-05 | NULL
1 | 2024-01-20 | 15
1 | 2024-02-01 | 12
2 | 2024-01-07 | NULL', '1 | 2024-01-05 | NULL
1 | 2024-01-20 | 13
1 | 2024-02-01 | 12
2 | 2024-01-07 | 2', '1 | 2024-01-05 | 0
1 | 2024-01-20 | 15
1 | 2024-02-01 | 12
2 | 2024-01-07 | 0', '1 | 2024-01-05 | -15
1 | 2024-01-20 | -12
1 | 2024-02-01 | NULL
2 | 2024-01-07 | NULL']::text[], 'SELECT customer_id, order_date,
       order_date - LAG(order_date) OVER (
         PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date
       ) AS gap
FROM orders
ORDER BY customer_id, order_date', '[{"name":"orders","columns":["customer_id","order_date"],"rows":[[1,"2024-01-05"],[1,"2024-01-20"],[2,"2024-01-07"],[1,"2024-02-01"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LAG returns the previous order_date within the same customer, and subtracting two dates gives a whole number of days. Each customer''s first order has no previous row, so LAG is NULL and so is the gap.', array['orders']::text[]),
(298, 'hard', 'write-sql', 'Subqueries', 'What''s the average number of orders per customer? In a derived table t, select customer_id and the per-customer count as order_count; then return AVG(order_count) AS avg_orders.', 'SELECT AVG(order_count) AS avg_orders FROM (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) t', array['SELECT AVG(order_count) AS avg_orders FROM (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(id) AS order_count FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) t', 'SELECT AVG(t.order_count) AS avg_orders FROM (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id) t']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Aggregate once inside FROM, then aggregate that result again.', 'Aggregates can''t be nested like AVG(COUNT(*)) in one query level. A derived table computes the per-customer counts first, and the outer query averages them.', array['orders']::text[]),
(299, 'hard', 'drag-drop', 'CASE', 'Show orders placed since 2024-01-01, pending ones first and newest first within each group, top 20.', 'SELECT id, status, order_date FROM orders WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01'' ORDER BY CASE WHEN status = ''pending'' THEN 0 ELSE 1 END, order_date DESC LIMIT 20', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT id, status, order_date', 'FROM orders', 'WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01''', 'ORDER BY CASE WHEN status = ''pending'' THEN 0 ELSE 1 END, order_date DESC', 'LIMIT 20']::text[], array['GROUP BY status']::text[], null, 'The CASE maps pending orders to 0 and everything else to 1, so sorting on it puts pending first, and order_date DESC then orders each group. LIMIT is applied after sorting.', array['orders']::text[]),
(300, 'hard', 'predict-output', 'Window Functions', 'What does this return for each user?', '1 | view
2 | view
3 | purchase', '{}'::text[], array['1 | view
2 | view
3 | purchase', '1 | login
2 | login
3 | purchase', '3 | purchase', '1 | login
2 | view
3 | purchase']::text[], 'SELECT user_id, event_type
FROM (
  SELECT user_id, event_type,
         ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY event_time DESC) AS rn
  FROM user_events
) t
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY user_id', '[{"name":"user_events","columns":["user_id","event_type","event_time"],"rows":[[1,"login","2024-03-01 09:00"],[1,"view","2024-03-01 09:05"],[2,"view","2024-03-01 08:00"],[2,"login","2024-03-01 07:55"],[3,"purchase","2024-03-02 10:00"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ROW_NUMBER restarts for each user_id, and ORDER BY event_time DESC gives 1 to each user''s latest event, so rn = 1 keeps one row per user. Row order in the table is irrelevant; only the window''s ORDER BY counts.', array['user_events']::text[])
on conflict (id) do nothing;

insert into public.questions (id, difficulty, type, topic, question, answer, alternatives, options, query, sample_tables, tokens, distractors, hint, explanation, schema_tables) values
(301, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'How long does a CTE defined in a WITH clause exist?', 'Only for the statement it belongs to', '{}'::text[], array['Only for the statement it belongs to', 'For the rest of the database session', 'Until the current transaction commits', 'Until it is removed with DROP VIEW']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A CTE is a named subquery scoped to one statement. To reuse a result across statements you need a view, a temporary table or a materialized view.', '{}'::text[]),
(302, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Generate the numbers 1 to 10 with a recursive CTE named nums whose only column is n: start at 1 and add 1 while n < 10. Return n.', 'WITH RECURSIVE nums AS (SELECT 1 AS n UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 10) SELECT n FROM nums', array['WITH RECURSIVE nums AS (SELECT 1 AS n UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 10) SELECT * FROM nums', 'WITH RECURSIVE nums(n) AS (SELECT 1 UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 10) SELECT n FROM nums']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Anchor row first, then UNION ALL a step that selects from nums.', 'The anchor SELECT 1 seeds the result. The recursive term adds n + 1 to the previous iteration''s rows until WHERE n < 10 returns nothing, which ends the recursion.', '{}'::text[]),
(303, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'CTEs', 'Arrange a query that uses a CTE to find departments whose average salary exceeds 80000, highest average first.', 'WITH dept_avg AS ( SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_sal FROM employees GROUP BY department ) SELECT department, avg_sal FROM dept_avg WHERE avg_sal > 80000 ORDER BY avg_sal DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH dept_avg AS (', 'SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_sal FROM employees', 'GROUP BY department', ')', 'SELECT department, avg_sal FROM dept_avg', 'WHERE avg_sal > 80000', 'ORDER BY avg_sal DESC']::text[], array['HAVING avg_sal > 80000']::text[], null, 'The CTE does the aggregation; the outer query sees avg_sal as an ordinary column, so a plain WHERE filters it. HAVING would need a GROUP BY in the outer query.', array['employees', 'departments']::text[]),
(304, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'What are the two parts of a recursive CTE, and what combines them?', 'Anchor term + recursive term, combined with UNION [ALL]', '{}'::text[], array['Anchor term + recursive term, combined with UNION [ALL]', 'Anchor term + recursive term, combined with a JOIN', 'Recursive term + stop condition, combined with WHERE', 'Two anchor terms, combined with INTERSECT']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The non-recursive anchor runs once to seed the result; the recursive term, which references the CTE itself, is then applied repeatedly. They must be combined with UNION or UNION ALL.', '{}'::text[]),
(305, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'CTEs', 'Finance wants big spenders. Write a CTE customer_totals with customer_id and SUM(total_amount) AS total_spent over ''delivered'' orders, grouped by customer_id. Then return customer_id and total_spent for totals above 1000, highest first.', 'WITH customer_totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS total_spent FROM orders WHERE status = ''delivered'' GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT customer_id, total_spent FROM customer_totals WHERE total_spent > 1000 ORDER BY total_spent DESC', array['WITH customer_totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS total_spent FROM orders WHERE status = ''delivered'' GROUP BY customer_id) SELECT * FROM customer_totals WHERE total_spent > 1000 ORDER BY total_spent DESC']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Filter orders inside the CTE; filter the totals in the outer query.', 'Inside the CTE, WHERE filters orders before grouping. In the outer query total_spent is just an ordinary column, so a plain WHERE (not HAVING) filters it.', array['orders']::text[]),
(306, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Recursive CTEs', 'This should return the numbers 1 to 5, but fails with: relation "nums" does not exist. Fix it.', 'WITH RECURSIVE nums AS (SELECT 1 AS n UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 5) SELECT n FROM nums', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH nums AS (SELECT 1 AS n UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 5) SELECT n FROM nums', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'PostgreSQL must be told this CTE refers to itself.', 'Without RECURSIVE, a CTE can''t reference its own name, so nums inside it is looked up as an ordinary table that doesn''t exist.', '{}'::text[]),
(307, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'Salaries ordered DESC are 100, 90, 90, 80. Which function gives 80 the value 3?', 'DENSE_RANK()', '{}'::text[], array['DENSE_RANK()', 'RANK()', 'ROW_NUMBER()', 'PERCENT_RANK()']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'RANK leaves a gap after the tie (1, 2, 2, 4) and ROW_NUMBER never ties (1, 2, 3, 4); DENSE_RANK continues with the next integer (1, 2, 2, 3). PERCENT_RANK would give 80 the value 1.', '{}'::text[]),
(308, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Arrange a recursive CTE that returns the numbers 1 to 10.', 'WITH RECURSIVE nums AS ( SELECT 1 AS n UNION ALL SELECT n + 1 FROM nums WHERE n < 10 ) SELECT n FROM nums', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH RECURSIVE nums AS (', 'SELECT 1 AS n', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT n + 1 FROM nums', 'WHERE n < 10', ')', 'SELECT n FROM nums']::text[], array['WITH nums AS (', 'WHERE n <= 10']::text[], null, 'The anchor comes first and only the recursive term references nums. The stop condition must sit in the recursive term: n < 10 makes 10 the last row (n <= 10 would add 11), while an outer WHERE would never stop the recursion.', '{}'::text[]),
(309, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'CTEs', 'What does this CTE query return?', 'Sales | 120000
Engineering | 90000', '{}'::text[], array['Sales | 120000
Engineering | 90000', 'Sales | 120000
Engineering | 90000
HR | 85000', 'Engineering | 90000', 'Engineering | 90000
Sales | 120000']::text[], 'WITH dept_totals AS (
  SELECT department, SUM(salary) AS total
  FROM employees
  GROUP BY department
)
SELECT department, total
FROM dept_totals
WHERE total > 85000
ORDER BY total DESC;', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["name","department","salary"],"rows":[["Ana","Sales",50000],["Ben","Sales",70000],["Cal","HR",40000],["Dee","HR",45000],["Eve","Engineering",90000]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The outer WHERE filters the CTE''s department totals (Sales 120000, Engineering 90000, HR 85000), not individual salaries. HR is excluded because 85000 is not > 85000.', array['employees']::text[]),
(310, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Ranking Functions', 'Rank products by price within each category, most expensive first, with no gaps after ties. Return name, category, price and the rank as price_rank.', 'SELECT name, category, price, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank FROM products', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One ranking function never skips a number after a tie.', 'DENSE_RANK gives tied rows the same rank and continues with the next integer (1, 1, 2), while RANK would skip (1, 1, 3). PARTITION BY restarts the numbering in each category.', array['products']::text[]),
(311, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'What is the grain of a fact table?', 'What one row represents, e.g. one sale line', '{}'::text[], array['What one row represents, e.g. one sale line', 'The number of dimensions joined to it', 'The most detailed column of each dimension', 'How often the table is refreshed']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Declaring the grain is the first step of dimensional design. Every measure and foreign key must be true at that grain; mixing grains in one table leads to double counting.', '{}'::text[]),
(312, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Ranking Functions', 'Rank employees by salary, highest first, as salary_rank. Fix the query.', 'SELECT name, salary, RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS salary_rank FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, salary, RANK(salary) OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS salary_rank FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The ranking column belongs somewhere other than the parentheses.', 'As a window function RANK() takes no arguments; the ORDER BY inside OVER decides what it ranks by. RANK(x) exists only as a hypothetical-set aggregate used with WITHIN GROUP.', array['employees']::text[]),
(313, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Data Warehouse', 'Arrange the query listing brands that sold more than 1000 units, best sellers first.', 'SELECT p.brand, SUM(f.quantity) AS units FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key GROUP BY p.brand HAVING SUM(f.quantity) > 1000 ORDER BY units DESC', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT p.brand, SUM(f.quantity) AS units', 'FROM fact_sales f', 'JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key', 'GROUP BY p.brand', 'HAVING SUM(f.quantity) > 1000', 'ORDER BY units DESC']::text[], array['WHERE SUM(f.quantity) > 1000']::text[], null, 'Filters on aggregated measures go in HAVING, after GROUP BY. WHERE filters individual fact rows and cannot contain aggregates.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_product']::text[]),
(314, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Ranking Functions', 'What do RANK and DENSE_RANK return here?', 'Ann | 1 | 1
Bo | 2 | 2
Cy | 2 | 2
Di | 4 | 3', '{}'::text[], array['Ann | 1 | 1
Bo | 2 | 2
Cy | 2 | 2
Di | 4 | 3', 'Ann | 1 | 1
Bo | 2 | 2
Cy | 2 | 2
Di | 3 | 3', 'Ann | 1 | 1
Bo | 2 | 2
Cy | 2 | 2
Di | 3 | 4', 'Ann | 1 | 1
Bo | 2 | 2
Cy | 3 | 3
Di | 4 | 4']::text[], 'SELECT player,
  RANK() OVER (ORDER BY points DESC) AS r,
  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY points DESC) AS dr
FROM scores
ORDER BY points DESC, player;', '[{"name":"scores","columns":["player","points"],"rows":[["Ann",90],["Bo",85],["Cy",85],["Di",70]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Bo and Cy tie, so both get 2 from each function. RANK then skips to 4 (two rows came before Di), while DENSE_RANK continues at 3.', '{}'::text[]),
(315, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Data Warehouse', 'Total revenue per store region, highest first. Alias fact_sales f and dim_store s, join on store_key, and return region and SUM(revenue) AS total_revenue.', 'SELECT s.region, SUM(f.revenue) AS total_revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key GROUP BY s.region ORDER BY total_revenue DESC', array['SELECT s.region, SUM(f.revenue) AS total_revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON s.store_key = f.store_key GROUP BY s.region ORDER BY total_revenue DESC', 'SELECT s.region, SUM(f.revenue) AS total_revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key GROUP BY s.region ORDER BY SUM(f.revenue) DESC']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Join the fact to the dimension on its key, then group by the attribute.', 'In a star schema the measures live in the fact table and the descriptive attributes in dimensions: join on the dimension''s key, then group by the attribute.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_store']::text[]),
(316, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Ranking Functions', 'Arrange the query that returns the single highest-paid employee in each department.', 'WITH ranked AS ( SELECT name, department, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn FROM employees ) SELECT name, department FROM ranked WHERE rn = 1', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH ranked AS (', 'SELECT name, department, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn', 'FROM employees', ')', 'SELECT name, department', 'FROM ranked', 'WHERE rn = 1']::text[], array['HAVING rn = 1', 'QUALIFY rn = 1']::text[], null, 'rn can''t be filtered in the query that defines it, so it is computed in a CTE and filtered outside with WHERE. PostgreSQL has no QUALIFY clause (that is Snowflake/BigQuery/Teradata).', array['employees']::text[]),
(317, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Data Warehouse', 'Units sold per product category in 2024. Fix the query.', 'SELECT p.category, SUM(f.quantity) AS units FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key WHERE d.year = 2024 GROUP BY p.category', array['SELECT p.category, SUM(f.quantity) AS units FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_date d ON d.date_key = f.date_key WHERE d.year = 2024 GROUP BY p.category']::text[], null, 'SELECT p.category, SUM(f.quantity) AS units FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_date d WHERE d.year = 2024 GROUP BY p.category', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Every JOIN except CROSS JOIN needs a join condition.', 'A JOIN without ON (or USING) is a syntax error in PostgreSQL; only CROSS JOIN has no condition. Fact rows link to dim_date through date_key.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_date', 'dim_product']::text[]),
(318, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'One row per day: which frame gives a trailing 3-day moving average (today and the two previous days)?', 'ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', '{}'::text[], array['ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', 'ROWS BETWEEN 3 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', 'ROWS BETWEEN CURRENT ROW AND 3 FOLLOWING', 'RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Two preceding rows plus the current row make three. 3 PRECEDING would average four rows, and UNBOUNDED PRECEDING gives a cumulative average rather than a moving one.', '{}'::text[]),
(319, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'In WITH a AS (...), b AS (...) SELECT ... (no RECURSIVE), which reference between the CTEs is allowed?', 'b may reference a, but a may not reference b', '{}'::text[], array['b may reference a, but a may not reference b', 'a may reference b, but b may not reference a', 'Neither CTE may reference the other', 'Each may reference the other freely']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Without RECURSIVE, each CTE can only see the CTEs defined before it in the list. That is what makes step-by-step CTE pipelines work.', '{}'::text[]),
(320, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Arrange a recursive CTE that walks UP the management chain from employee 42 to the CEO, returning each name.', 'WITH RECURSIVE chain AS ( SELECT id, name, manager_id FROM employees WHERE id = 42 UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id FROM employees e JOIN chain c ON e.id = c.manager_id ) SELECT name FROM chain', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH RECURSIVE chain AS (', 'SELECT id, name, manager_id FROM employees WHERE id = 42', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT e.id, e.name, e.manager_id', 'FROM employees e JOIN chain c ON e.id = c.manager_id', ')', 'SELECT name FROM chain']::text[], array['FROM employees e JOIN chain c ON e.manager_id = c.id']::text[], null, 'Walking up means the next row is the current row''s manager: e.id = c.manager_id. The reverse condition, e.manager_id = c.id, walks down to subordinates instead.', array['employees']::text[]),
(321, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'Why does this query fail?', 'Several CTEs need one WITH, separated by commas', '{}'::text[], array['Several CTEs need one WITH, separated by commas', 'A query may define only one CTE', 'CTEs must be combined with JOIN, not a comma', 'Each CTE needs its own RECURSIVE keyword']::text[], 'WITH a AS (SELECT 1 AS x)
WITH b AS (SELECT 2 AS y)
SELECT * FROM a, b;', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Write WITH once and list the CTEs with commas: WITH a AS (...), b AS (...). A second WITH keyword at that point is a syntax error.', '{}'::text[]),
(322, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Recursive CTEs', 'List everyone who reports to employee 1, directly or indirectly. Recursive CTE reports: the anchor selects id, name of employees with manager_id = 1; the recursive part selects e.id, e.name from employees e joined to reports r on e.manager_id = r.id. Return id, name.', 'WITH RECURSIVE reports AS (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 1 UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name FROM employees e JOIN reports r ON e.manager_id = r.id) SELECT id, name FROM reports', array['WITH RECURSIVE reports AS (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 1 UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name FROM employees e JOIN reports r ON e.manager_id = r.id) SELECT * FROM reports', 'WITH RECURSIVE reports AS (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 1 UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name FROM employees e JOIN reports r ON r.id = e.manager_id) SELECT id, name FROM reports']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Each round finds people whose manager is in the previous round.', 'Each iteration finds employees whose manager was found in the previous iteration, walking down the tree one level at a time until no one new reports to the last level.', array['employees']::text[]),
(323, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'A CTE contains ORDER BY salary DESC, and the outer query selects from it with no ORDER BY. What is guaranteed about the output order?', 'Nothing, unless the outer query has ORDER BY', '{}'::text[], array['Nothing, unless the outer query has ORDER BY', 'Rows are always returned by salary DESC', 'Rows are returned in insertion order', 'PostgreSQL rejects ORDER BY inside a CTE']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'SQL guarantees order only for the query that carries the ORDER BY. An inner sort may seem to survive, but joins, filters or plan changes can reorder rows, so sort the final SELECT.', '{}'::text[]),
(324, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Recursive CTEs', 'How many people sit at each level of this org chart?', '1 | 1
2 | 2
3 | 1
4 | 1', '{}'::text[], array['1 | 1
2 | 2
3 | 1
4 | 1', '1 | 1
2 | 2
3 | 2', '0 | 1
1 | 2
2 | 1
3 | 1', '1 | 1
2 | 4']::text[], 'WITH RECURSIVE org AS (
  SELECT id, 1 AS level FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL
  UNION ALL
  SELECT e.id, o.level + 1
  FROM employees e JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id
)
SELECT level, COUNT(*) AS people
FROM org
GROUP BY level
ORDER BY level;', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["id","name","manager_id"],"rows":[[1,"Ava",null],[2,"Ben",1],[3,"Cid",1],[4,"Dot",2],[5,"Eli",4]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Ava (no manager) is level 1, Ben and Cid report to her (2), Dot reports to Ben (3) and Eli to Dot (4). Each recursive step adds exactly one level.', array['employees']::text[]),
(325, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'Why does this query fail?', 'WHERE runs before window functions are computed', '{}'::text[], array['WHERE runs before window functions are computed', 'ROW_NUMBER() requires a PARTITION BY clause', 'Column aliases can never be used in filters', 'Window results must be filtered with HAVING']::text[], 'SELECT name, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn
FROM employees
WHERE rn <= 5;', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The logical order is FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, then window functions and SELECT aliases, then ORDER BY. Compute rn in a CTE or subquery and filter it in the outer query.', array['employees']::text[]),
(326, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'CTEs', 'Arrange chained CTEs that count how many customers placed more than 5 orders.', 'WITH order_counts AS ( SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id ), frequent AS ( SELECT customer_id FROM order_counts WHERE n > 5 ) SELECT COUNT(*) AS frequent_customers FROM frequent', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH order_counts AS (', 'SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id', '), frequent AS (', 'SELECT customer_id FROM order_counts WHERE n > 5', ')', 'SELECT COUNT(*) AS frequent_customers', 'FROM frequent']::text[], array['SELECT customer_id FROM orders WHERE COUNT(*) > 5']::text[], null, 'The first CTE counts orders per customer; the second can read it and filter on n with WHERE, since n is now a plain column. The final query counts the surviving rows.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(327, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'When does a PostgreSQL recursive CTE stop iterating?', 'When the recursive term returns no new rows', '{}'::text[], array['When the recursive term returns no new rows', 'After a default limit of 100 iterations', 'When the anchor term returns no rows', 'When the outer query applies ORDER BY']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Each iteration runs the recursive term on the previous iteration''s rows; zero rows ends it. PostgreSQL has no iteration cap (unlike SQL Server''s MAXRECURSION 100), so a missing stop condition can run forever.', '{}'::text[]),
(328, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'CTEs', 'Using a CTE named dept_avg (department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary per department), list the name and salary of employees who earn more than their department''s average. Alias employees e and dept_avg d and join on department.', 'WITH dept_avg AS (SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary FROM employees GROUP BY department) SELECT e.name, e.salary FROM employees e JOIN dept_avg d ON e.department = d.department WHERE e.salary > d.avg_salary', array['WITH dept_avg AS (SELECT department, AVG(salary) AS avg_salary FROM employees GROUP BY department) SELECT e.name, e.salary FROM employees e JOIN dept_avg d ON d.department = e.department WHERE e.salary > d.avg_salary']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Build the averages first, then join each employee to their department''s row.', 'The CTE computes one average per department; joining it back lets each employee be compared with their own department''s figure. WHERE salary > AVG(salary) is illegal because aggregates can''t appear in WHERE.', array['employees']::text[]),
(329, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'Two Sales employees tie on salary. With ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC), which one gets rn = 1?', 'Either; the tie order is not guaranteed', '{}'::text[], array['Either; the tie order is not guaranteed', 'The one with the lower primary key', 'The one inserted into the table first', 'Both of them share rn = 1']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ROW_NUMBER must hand out distinct numbers, but for tied sort keys the order is arbitrary and can change between runs. Add a tiebreaker such as ORDER BY salary DESC, id to make it deterministic.', array['employees']::text[]),
(330, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'CTEs', 'This should chain two CTEs (shipped orders, then shipped-order counts per customer) and return customers with more than 3. Fix it.', 'WITH shipped AS (SELECT id, customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''shipped''),
per_customer AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM shipped GROUP BY customer_id)
SELECT customer_id, order_count FROM per_customer WHERE order_count > 3', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH shipped AS (SELECT id, customer_id FROM orders WHERE status = ''shipped'')
per_customer AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS order_count FROM shipped GROUP BY customer_id)
SELECT customer_id, order_count FROM per_customer WHERE order_count > 3', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Look at how the two CTE definitions are separated.', 'CTEs in one WITH clause are separated by commas. Without it PostgreSQL hits per_customer right after the first CTE and raises a syntax error.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(331, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Ranking Functions', 'Who is the top rep in each region?', 'East | Lee
West | Ned', '{}'::text[], array['East | Lee
West | Ned', 'East | Lee
West | Max', 'East | Lee
West | Max
West | Ned', 'East | Kim
West | Ola']::text[], 'SELECT region, rep FROM (
  SELECT region, rep,
    ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY region
      ORDER BY amount DESC, rep DESC) AS rn
  FROM sales_reps
) t
WHERE rn = 1
ORDER BY region;', '[{"name":"sales_reps","columns":["rep","region","amount"],"rows":[["Kim","East",500],["Lee","East",700],["Max","West",700],["Ned","West",700],["Ola","West",300]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Max and Ned tie on 700 in West, so the tiebreaker rep DESC decides: Ned sorts before Max. ROW_NUMBER still returns exactly one row per region.', '{}'::text[]),
(332, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'A customer moves from segment ''Consumer'' to ''Corporate''. You insert a new dim_customer row with valid_from / valid_to dates and keep the old row. Which SCD type is this?', 'Type 2', '{}'::text[], array['Type 2', 'Type 1', 'Type 3', 'Type 0']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'SCD Type 2 preserves history by adding a new row (with a new surrogate key) per version, so old facts still point at the old segment. Type 1 overwrites; Type 3 keeps a ''previous value'' column.', array['dim_customer']::text[]),
(333, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Ranking Functions', 'Arrange the query ranking in-stock products by price within each category (ties share a rank, gaps allowed), sorted by category and rank.', 'SELECT name, category, price, RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank FROM products WHERE stock > 0 ORDER BY category, price_rank', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT name, category, price,', 'RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank', 'FROM products', 'WHERE stock > 0', 'ORDER BY category, price_rank']::text[], array['WHERE price_rank <= 3', 'DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS price_rank']::text[], null, 'RANK leaves gaps after ties (1, 1, 3), which is what was asked. WHERE filters rows before ranking, while ORDER BY runs last and may use the window alias.', array['products']::text[]),
(334, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'CTEs', 'Which customers does this chained-CTE query return?', '1 | 350
2 | 400', '{}'::text[], array['1 | 350
2 | 400', '1 | 350
2 | 400
3 | 200', '2 | 400', '1 | 250
2 | 400']::text[], 'WITH totals AS (
  SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent
  FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id
), stats AS (
  SELECT AVG(spent) AS avg_spent FROM totals
)
SELECT t.customer_id, t.spent
FROM totals t, stats s
WHERE t.spent > s.avg_spent
ORDER BY t.customer_id;', '[{"name":"orders","columns":["customer_id","total_amount"],"rows":[[1,100],[1,250],[2,400],[3,90],[3,110]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'stats averages the three customer totals (350, 400, 200), giving about 316.67, so customer 3 is below it. Averaging the five raw orders (190) would wrongly include customer 3.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(335, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Ranking Functions', 'Return the top 3 earners per department. In a CTE ranked, select name, department, salary and ROW_NUMBER() per department by salary DESC as rn; then return name, department, salary where rn <= 3.', 'WITH ranked AS (SELECT name, department, salary, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn FROM employees) SELECT name, department, salary FROM ranked WHERE rn <= 3', array['WITH ranked AS (SELECT name, department, salary, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rn FROM employees) SELECT name, department, salary FROM ranked WHERE rn < 4']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'A window result can only be filtered one query level up.', 'Window functions are evaluated after WHERE, so rn must be computed in the CTE and filtered outside it. ROW_NUMBER returns at most 3 rows per department even when salaries tie.', array['employees']::text[]),
(336, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'What makes dim_date a conformed dimension?', 'Several fact tables share it with the same keys', '{}'::text[], array['Several fact tables share it with the same keys', 'It is normalized into year, quarter and month tables', 'Each of its rows passes a CHECK constraint', 'It has exactly one row per fact row']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A conformed dimension is reused identically across fact tables and data marts, so measures from different processes (sales, returns, inventory) can be compared on the same calendar.', array['dim_date']::text[]),
(337, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Ranking Functions', 'Number employees within each department by hire date (earliest = 1) as seniority. Fix the query.', 'SELECT name, department, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY department ORDER BY hire_date) AS seniority FROM employees', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT name, department, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY hire_date PARTITION BY department) AS seniority FROM employees', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Clauses inside OVER have a fixed order.', 'Inside OVER the clauses have a fixed order: PARTITION BY, then ORDER BY, then an optional frame clause.', array['employees']::text[]),
(338, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Data Warehouse', 'Arrange the query showing revenue by region and city, with a subtotal per region and a grand total (no city-only subtotals).', 'SELECT s.region, s.city, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key GROUP BY ROLLUP (s.region, s.city) ORDER BY s.region, s.city', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT s.region, s.city, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue', 'FROM fact_sales f', 'JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key', 'GROUP BY ROLLUP (s.region, s.city)', 'ORDER BY s.region, s.city']::text[], array['GROUP BY CUBE (s.region, s.city)', 'GROUP BY ROLLUP (s.city, s.region)']::text[], null, 'ROLLUP follows the hierarchy left to right: (region, city), (region), (). CUBE would add city-only subtotals, and reversing the columns would subtotal per city instead of per region.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_store']::text[]),
(339, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Business Scenarios', 'Daily revenue with a running total. From orders grouped by order_date, return order_date, SUM(total_amount) AS daily_revenue, and the cumulative sum of the daily totals ordered by order_date AS running_total.', 'SELECT order_date, SUM(total_amount) AS daily_revenue, SUM(SUM(total_amount)) OVER (ORDER BY order_date) AS running_total FROM orders GROUP BY order_date', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'A window function can wrap an aggregate; the inner one runs per group.', 'GROUP BY first collapses orders into one row per day; the window SUM(SUM(total_amount)) then accumulates those daily totals. The window can''t reference the daily_revenue alias, hence the nested aggregate.', array['orders']::text[]),
(340, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Ranking Functions', 'Arrange the query that finds the products at the second-highest price level in each category.', 'SELECT category, name, price FROM ( SELECT category, name, price, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS dr FROM products ) t WHERE dr = 2', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT category, name, price', 'FROM (', 'SELECT category, name, price, DENSE_RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS dr', 'FROM products', ') t', 'WHERE dr = 2']::text[], array['WHERE DENSE_RANK() = 2']::text[], null, 'DENSE_RANK makes the second distinct price rank 2 even if the top price is tied. The window must be computed in a subquery before the outer WHERE can filter on it.', array['products']::text[]),
(341, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Data Warehouse', 'Revenue per store region and customer segment. Fix the query.', 'SELECT s.region, c.segment, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key GROUP BY s.region, c.segment', array['SELECT s.region, c.segment, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key GROUP BY c.segment, s.region']::text[], null, 'SELECT s.region, c.segment, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key GROUP BY s.region', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Every non-aggregated SELECT column must be grouped.', 'Every non-aggregated column in SELECT must appear in GROUP BY. c.segment is missing, so PostgreSQL can''t decide which segment to show per region.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_store', 'dim_customer']::text[]),
(342, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'For a monthly retention report, how is each customer''s cohort usually defined from orders?', 'DATE_TRUNC(''month'', MIN(order_date)) per customer', '{}'::text[], array['DATE_TRUNC(''month'', MIN(order_date)) per customer', 'DATE_TRUNC(''month'', MAX(order_date)) per customer', 'DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) for each order', 'The month of the customer''s largest order']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A cohort groups customers by when they started, so use each customer''s first order month. Retention then measures how many of each cohort are active in later months.', array['orders']::text[]),
(343, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Data Warehouse', 'What does this ROLLUP return?', 'East | 300
West | 150
NULL | 450', '{}'::text[], array['East | 300
West | 150
NULL | 450', 'East | 300
West | 150', 'NULL | 450
East | 300
West | 150', 'East | 100
East | 200
West | 150
NULL | 450']::text[], 'SELECT region, SUM(revenue) AS total
FROM sales
GROUP BY ROLLUP (region)
ORDER BY region;', '[{"name":"sales","columns":["region","revenue"],"rows":[["East",100],["East",200],["West",150]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'ROLLUP (region) adds a grand-total row whose region is NULL. In ascending order PostgreSQL sorts NULLs last, so the total appears at the bottom.', '{}'::text[]),
(344, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'There are 200 orders and 25 are cancelled. What does this return?', '0', '{}'::text[], array['0', '12.5', '12', '13']::text[], 'SELECT COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE status = ''cancelled'')
       / COUNT(*) * 100 AS cancel_pct
FROM orders;', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Both counts are bigint, so 25 / 200 is integer division and gives 0 before the * 100. Write 100.0 * COUNT(...) / COUNT(*) to get 12.5.', array['orders']::text[]),
(345, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Data Warehouse', 'Revenue by year and quarter with yearly subtotals and a grand total. Alias fact_sales f and dim_date d, join on date_key, return year, quarter and SUM(revenue) AS revenue using ROLLUP. No ORDER BY.', 'SELECT d.year, d.quarter, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key GROUP BY ROLLUP (d.year, d.quarter)', array['SELECT d.year, d.quarter, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON d.date_key = f.date_key GROUP BY ROLLUP (d.year, d.quarter)']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'ROLLUP builds subtotals by dropping columns from the right.', 'ROLLUP (year, quarter) produces the grouping sets (year, quarter), (year) and (): detail rows, one subtotal per year and a grand total. Rolled-up columns appear as NULL.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_date']::text[]),
(346, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Business Scenarios', '7-day moving average of daily revenue. Fix the query.', 'WITH daily AS (SELECT order_date, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY order_date) SELECT order_date, AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY order_date ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW) AS ma_7 FROM daily', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH daily AS (SELECT order_date, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY order_date) SELECT order_date, AVG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY order_date ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROWS) AS ma_7 FROM daily', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Check the exact spelling of the frame''s end bound.', 'The frame bound is spelled CURRENT ROW. Six preceding rows plus the current one form the 7-row window.', array['orders']::text[]),
(347, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Business Scenarios', 'What month-over-month changes does this return?', '2024-01-01 | NULL
2024-02-01 | 30
2024-03-01 | -10', '{}'::text[], array['2024-01-01 | NULL
2024-02-01 | 30
2024-03-01 | -10', '2024-01-01 | 0
2024-02-01 | 30
2024-03-01 | -10', '2024-01-01 | 30
2024-02-01 | -10
2024-03-01 | NULL', '2024-01-01 | 100
2024-02-01 | 30
2024-03-01 | -10']::text[], 'SELECT month,
  revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS change
FROM monthly
ORDER BY month;', '[{"name":"monthly","columns":["month","revenue"],"rows":[["2024-01-01",100],["2024-02-01",130],["2024-03-01",120]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LAG returns NULL for the first row (no previous month), and anything minus NULL is NULL. Use LAG(revenue, 1, 0) or COALESCE if you want 0 instead; LEAD would look forward.', '{}'::text[]),
(348, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Business Scenarios', 'Arrange a statement that deletes duplicate customers (same email), keeping the row with the lowest id.', 'DELETE FROM customers WHERE id IN ( SELECT id FROM (SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn FROM customers) t WHERE rn > 1 )', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['DELETE FROM customers', 'WHERE id IN (', 'SELECT id FROM (SELECT id, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY email ORDER BY id) AS rn FROM customers) t', 'WHERE rn > 1', ')']::text[], array['WHERE rn = 1']::text[], null, 'ROW_NUMBER numbers each email''s rows starting from the lowest id, so every row with rn > 1 is a duplicate. Deleting rn = 1 instead would remove the rows you meant to keep.', array['customers']::text[]),
(349, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'Which column names can the outer query use from t?', 'dept and n', '{}'::text[], array['dept and n', 'department and count', 'department and n', 'only dept, since COUNT(*) has no alias']::text[], 'WITH t(dept, n) AS (
  SELECT department, COUNT(*)
  FROM employees
  GROUP BY department
)
SELECT * FROM t;', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'A column list after the CTE name renames its output columns by position, overriding the names the inner SELECT produced (department and count).', array['employees']::text[]),
(350, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Recursive CTEs', 'How many people are in Noah''s (id 2) reporting tree?', '3', '{}'::text[], array['3', '2', '4', '5']::text[], 'WITH RECURSIVE sub AS (
  SELECT id FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 2
  UNION ALL
  SELECT e.id
  FROM employees e JOIN sub s ON e.manager_id = s.id
)
SELECT COUNT(*) FROM sub;', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["id","name","manager_id"],"rows":[[1,"Mia",null],[2,"Noah",1],[3,"Omar",2],[4,"Pia",2],[5,"Quinn",3],[6,"Rosa",1]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The anchor finds Noah''s direct reports (Omar, Pia); the recursion adds Quinn, who reports to Omar. Noah himself is not included because the anchor starts one level below him.', array['employees']::text[])
on conflict (id) do nothing;

insert into public.questions (id, difficulty, type, topic, question, answer, alternatives, options, query, sample_tables, tokens, distractors, hint, explanation, schema_tables) values
(351, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'NTILE(4) is applied to 10 rows. How many rows land in buckets 1, 2, 3 and 4?', '3, 3, 2, 2', '{}'::text[], array['3, 3, 2, 2', '2, 2, 3, 3', '3, 2, 3, 2', '2, 2, 2, 4']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'NTILE splits rows as evenly as possible and gives the leftover rows to the first buckets: 10 / 4 is 2 remainder 2, so buckets 1 and 2 get one extra row each.', '{}'::text[]),
(352, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'CTEs', 'What percentages does this query return?', 'South | 40
East | 30
North | 30', '{}'::text[], array['South | 40
East | 30
North | 30', 'South | 40
North | 30
East | 30', 'South | 100
East | 100
North | 100', 'South | 40
East | 30
North | 20
North | 10']::text[], 'WITH r AS (
  SELECT region, SUM(amount) AS amt
  FROM sales GROUP BY region
)
SELECT region,
  ROUND(100.0 * amt / (SELECT SUM(amt) FROM r), 1) AS pct
FROM r
ORDER BY pct DESC, region;', '[{"name":"sales","columns":["region","amount"],"rows":[["North",100],["North",50],["South",200],["East",150]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The CTE is referenced twice: once as the row source and once in the scalar subquery for the grand total (500). East and North tie at 30%, so the second sort key, region, puts East first.', '{}'::text[]),
(353, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'In a recursive CTE, what does using UNION instead of UNION ALL do?', 'Discards rows already produced, which can stop cycles', '{}'::text[], array['Discards rows already produced, which can stop cycles', 'Nothing, recursive CTEs always keep duplicates', 'Runs the anchor and recursive terms in parallel', 'Switches the search from breadth-first to depth-first']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'With UNION, a new row is dropped if it duplicates any row already produced, so revisiting the same rows ends the loop. It only helps when repeated rows are fully identical: a growing level or path column defeats it.', '{}'::text[]),
(354, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'CTEs', 'This should total revenue per customer for orders since 2024 using the recent CTE. Fix it.', 'WITH recent AS (SELECT id, customer_id, total_amount FROM orders WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01'')
SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM recent GROUP BY customer_id', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH recent AS (SELECT id, customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01'')
SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM recent GROUP BY customer_id', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The outer query can only see the columns the CTE selects.', 'A CTE exposes only the columns in its own SELECT list, not every column of the underlying table. Add total_amount to the CTE so the outer SUM can see it.', array['orders']::text[]),
(355, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Bad data has created a management cycle (A manages B, B manages A). How do you stop an org-chart recursive CTE from looping forever?', 'Track visited ids in an array and skip repeats', '{}'::text[], array['Track visited ids in an array and skip repeats', 'Change the recursive join to a LEFT JOIN', 'Add ORDER BY id inside the recursive term', 'Use SELECT DISTINCT in the anchor term']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Accumulating a path (ARRAY[id], then path || e.id) lets the recursive term refuse ids it has already visited. PostgreSQL 14+ also has a built-in CYCLE clause that does the same bookkeeping.', '{}'::text[]),
(356, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'CTEs', 'The CTE has its own ORDER BY and LIMIT. What does the outer query return?', 'Electronics | 2
Home | 1', '{}'::text[], array['Electronics | 2
Home | 1', 'Books | 1
Electronics | 2
Home | 2', 'Books | 1
Electronics | 1
Home | 1', 'Home | 1
Electronics | 2']::text[], 'WITH top3 AS (
  SELECT name, category FROM products
  ORDER BY price DESC
  LIMIT 3
)
SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS n
FROM top3
GROUP BY category
ORDER BY category;', '[{"name":"products","columns":["name","category","price"],"rows":[["Laptop","Electronics",1200],["Phone","Electronics",800],["Sofa","Home",900],["Novel","Books",20],["Lamp","Home",60]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LIMIT 3 inside the CTE keeps only the three most expensive products (Laptop, Sofa, Phone) before the outer query groups them. The outer ORDER BY then sorts the categories alphabetically.', array['products']::text[]),
(357, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Arrange a query that returns the names of employees at level 3 of the org chart (the CEO is level 1).', 'WITH RECURSIVE org AS ( SELECT id, name, 1 AS lvl FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name, o.lvl + 1 FROM employees e JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id ) SELECT name FROM org WHERE lvl = 3', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH RECURSIVE org AS (', 'SELECT id, name, 1 AS lvl FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT e.id, e.name, o.lvl + 1 FROM employees e', 'JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id', ')', 'SELECT name FROM org', 'WHERE lvl = 3']::text[], array['WHERE manager_id = NULL']::text[], null, 'The anchor seeds the CEO (manager_id IS NULL) at level 1, and each recursive step adds one. Filtering lvl = 3 in the outer query keeps just that layer; = NULL would never match the CEO.', array['employees']::text[]),
(358, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'CTEs', 'The CTE should keep customers with at least 5 orders; the outer query counts them. Fix it.', 'WITH loyal AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id HAVING COUNT(*) >= 5)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS loyal_customers FROM loyal', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH loyal AS (SELECT customer_id, COUNT(*) AS n FROM orders WHERE COUNT(*) >= 5 GROUP BY customer_id)
SELECT COUNT(*) AS loyal_customers FROM loyal', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Aggregates can''t be filtered before the groups exist.', 'WHERE runs before GROUP BY, so it can''t use COUNT(*). Conditions on groups go in HAVING, after GROUP BY (and PostgreSQL won''t accept the alias n there either).', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(359, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'You need the top 3 products by price in each category, and any product tied with the 3rd one must also be returned. Which approach fits?', 'RANK() ... AS r, then WHERE r <= 3', '{}'::text[], array['RANK() ... AS r, then WHERE r <= 3', 'ROW_NUMBER() ... AS r, then WHERE r <= 3', 'NTILE(3) ... AS r, then WHERE r = 1', 'COUNT(*) ... AS r, then WHERE r <= 3']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'RANK gives tied rows the same rank, so r <= 3 keeps everything tied at the cutoff. ROW_NUMBER would cut ties off arbitrarily at exactly three rows.', array['products']::text[]),
(360, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'CTEs', 'Arrange a query that lists customers (id, name) with no orders since 2024-01-01, using a CTE of active customers.', 'WITH active AS ( SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01'' ) SELECT c.id, c.name FROM customers c LEFT JOIN active a ON c.id = a.customer_id WHERE a.customer_id IS NULL', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH active AS (', 'SELECT DISTINCT customer_id FROM orders WHERE order_date >= ''2024-01-01''', ')', 'SELECT c.id, c.name', 'FROM customers c', 'LEFT JOIN active a ON c.id = a.customer_id', 'WHERE a.customer_id IS NULL']::text[], array['WHERE a.customer_id = NULL', 'JOIN active a ON c.id = a.customer_id']::text[], null, 'LEFT JOIN keeps every customer, and customers with no active match get NULLs, which IS NULL picks out (an anti-join). An inner JOIN would drop them, and = NULL is never true.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(361, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Ranking Functions', 'How many products per category survive the rank filter?', 'Home | 3
Toys | 2', '{}'::text[], array['Home | 3
Toys | 2', 'Home | 2
Toys | 2', 'Home | 3
Toys | 1', 'Home | 4
Toys | 2']::text[], 'WITH r AS (
  SELECT name, category,
    RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY category ORDER BY price DESC) AS rk
  FROM products
)
SELECT category, COUNT(*)
FROM r
WHERE rk <= 2
GROUP BY category
ORDER BY category;', '[{"name":"products","columns":["name","category","price"],"rows":[["Mug","Home",15],["Vase","Home",30],["Rug","Home",30],["Lamp","Home",45],["Kite","Toys",20],["Ball","Toys",8]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'In Home, Lamp is rank 1 and Vase and Rug tie at 2 (Mug is 4), so three rows pass rk <= 2. RANK keeps ties at the cutoff, unlike ROW_NUMBER.', array['products']::text[]),
(362, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'CTEs', 'Chain two CTEs: totals (customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent from orders, per customer) and avg_spend (AVG(spent) AS avg_spent from totals). Return customer_id and spent for customers above that average, using totals CROSS JOIN avg_spend.', 'WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id), avg_spend AS (SELECT AVG(spent) AS avg_spent FROM totals) SELECT customer_id, spent FROM totals CROSS JOIN avg_spend WHERE spent > avg_spent', array['WITH totals AS (SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id), avg_spend AS (SELECT AVG(spent) AS avg_spent FROM totals) SELECT customer_id, spent FROM totals, avg_spend WHERE spent > avg_spent']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'The second CTE can read from the first; separate them with a comma.', 'A later CTE can read an earlier one, so avg_spend averages per-customer totals rather than individual orders. Cross joining a one-row CTE attaches that average to every row for comparison.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(363, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Recursive CTEs', 'This should list everyone under manager 7 at every level, but fails with an ambiguous-column error. Fix it.', 'WITH RECURSIVE team AS (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 7 UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name FROM employees e JOIN team t ON e.manager_id = t.id) SELECT id, name FROM team', '{}'::text[], null, 'WITH RECURSIVE team AS (SELECT id, name FROM employees WHERE manager_id = 7 UNION ALL SELECT id, name FROM employees e JOIN team t ON e.manager_id = t.id) SELECT id, name FROM team', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Both sources in the recursive join have id and name columns.', 'In the recursive term both employees e and the CTE team have id and name columns, so bare names are ambiguous. Qualifying them with e. takes the new level''s rows from employees.', array['employees']::text[]),
(364, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Ranking Functions', 'Arrange the query that returns id and name of employees in the top salary decile (bucket 1 = highest salaries).', 'WITH deciles AS ( SELECT id, name, NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS decile FROM employees ) SELECT id, name FROM deciles WHERE decile = 1', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH deciles AS (', 'SELECT id, name,', 'NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS decile', 'FROM employees', ')', 'SELECT id, name FROM deciles', 'WHERE decile = 1']::text[], array['NTILE(10) OVER (ORDER BY salary) AS decile']::text[], null, 'NTILE(10) splits the ordered rows into 10 near-equal buckets; ordering by salary DESC makes bucket 1 the top earners. Ascending order would put the lowest earners in bucket 1.', array['employees']::text[]),
(365, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'Which of these would be a non-additive measure if stored in fact_sales?', 'margin_pct (profit / revenue per line)', '{}'::text[], array['margin_pct (profit / revenue per line)', 'revenue per line', 'quantity per line', 'cost per line']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Revenue, cost and quantity can be summed across any dimension, but summing ratios is meaningless. Compute margin after aggregating: SUM(revenue - cost) / SUM(revenue).', array['fact_sales']::text[]),
(366, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Business Scenarios', 'What running totals does this produce?', '2024-01-01 | 10 | 10
2024-01-02 | 5 | 35
2024-01-02 | 20 | 35
2024-01-03 | 15 | 50', '{}'::text[], array['2024-01-01 | 10 | 10
2024-01-02 | 5 | 35
2024-01-02 | 20 | 35
2024-01-03 | 15 | 50', '2024-01-01 | 10 | 10
2024-01-02 | 5 | 15
2024-01-02 | 20 | 35
2024-01-03 | 15 | 50', '2024-01-01 | 10 | 10
2024-01-02 | 5 | 25
2024-01-02 | 20 | 25
2024-01-03 | 15 | 15', '2024-01-01 | 10 | 50
2024-01-02 | 5 | 50
2024-01-02 | 20 | 50
2024-01-03 | 15 | 50']::text[], 'SELECT pay_date, amount,
  SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY pay_date) AS running
FROM payments
ORDER BY pay_date, amount;', '[{"name":"payments","columns":["pay_date","amount"],"rows":[["2024-01-01",10],["2024-01-02",20],["2024-01-02",5],["2024-01-03",15]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The default frame with ORDER BY is RANGE ... CURRENT ROW, which includes all rows sharing the current pay_date. Both 2024-01-02 payments therefore show 35, the total through the end of that day.', '{}'::text[]),
(367, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Show org-chart depth. Recursive CTE org: the anchor is the CEO (manager_id IS NULL) with id, name and 1 AS level; the recursive part joins employees e to org o on e.manager_id = o.id, adding o.level + 1. Return name, level ordered by level.', 'WITH RECURSIVE org AS (SELECT id, name, 1 AS level FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name, o.level + 1 FROM employees e JOIN org o ON e.manager_id = o.id) SELECT name, level FROM org ORDER BY level', array['WITH RECURSIVE org AS (SELECT id, name, 1 AS level FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL UNION ALL SELECT e.id, e.name, o.level + 1 FROM employees e JOIN org o ON o.id = e.manager_id) SELECT name, level FROM org ORDER BY level']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Carry a level column and add one to it in the recursive step.', 'Carrying a counter through the recursion records how many hops each employee is from the root. The anchor fixes the CEO at level 1 and every recursive step adds one.', array['employees']::text[]),
(368, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Ranking Functions', 'List employees whose salary is among the 5 highest distinct salaries. Fix the query.', 'WITH r AS (SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) AS rnk FROM employees) SELECT name, salary FROM r WHERE rnk <= 5', array['WITH r AS (SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM employees) SELECT name, salary FROM r WHERE dense_rank <= 5']::text[], null, 'WITH r AS (SELECT name, salary, DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC) FROM employees) SELECT name, salary FROM r WHERE rnk <= 5', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'What is the ranking column actually called inside the CTE?', 'An unaliased window column is named after its function (dense_rank), so rnk doesn''t exist. Give the column the alias the outer query expects.', array['employees']::text[]),
(369, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'Some stores have a NULL region. How can you tell the ROLLUP grand-total row from the real NULL-region group?', 'GROUPING(region) is 1 only on the rolled-up row', '{}'::text[], array['GROUPING(region) is 1 only on the rolled-up row', 'The grand-total row always sorts last', 'COALESCE(region, ''Total'') labels only the total', 'ROLLUP drops rows whose region is NULL']::text[], 'SELECT region, SUM(revenue)
FROM sales_by_store
GROUP BY ROLLUP (region);', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'GROUPING(col) returns 1 when col was aggregated away by ROLLUP, CUBE or GROUPING SETS and 0 otherwise. COALESCE can''t tell the two NULLs apart and would label both.', '{}'::text[]),
(370, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Business Scenarios', 'Which login streaks does this gaps-and-islands query find?', '2024-06-01 | 3
2024-06-05 | 2', '{}'::text[], array['2024-06-01 | 3
2024-06-05 | 2', '2024-06-01 | 5', '2024-06-03 | 3
2024-06-06 | 2', '2024-06-01 | 3
2024-06-05 | 1']::text[], 'WITH g AS (
  SELECT login_date,
    login_date - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (ORDER BY login_date)::int AS grp
  FROM logins
)
SELECT MIN(login_date) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak_days
FROM g
GROUP BY grp
ORDER BY start_day;', '[{"name":"logins","columns":["login_date"],"rows":[["2024-06-01"],["2024-06-02"],["2024-06-03"],["2024-06-05"],["2024-06-06"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Date minus row number is 2024-05-31 for June 1-3 and 2024-06-01 for June 5-6, so the gap on June 4 splits two islands. MIN gives each streak''s first day and COUNT its length.', '{}'::text[]),
(371, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Data Warehouse', 'Arrange the query returning 2024 weekend revenue per product category.', 'SELECT p.category, SUM(f.revenue) AS weekend_revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key WHERE d.year = 2024 AND d.is_weekend GROUP BY p.category', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT p.category, SUM(f.revenue) AS weekend_revenue', 'FROM fact_sales f', 'JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key', 'WHERE d.year = 2024 AND d.is_weekend', 'GROUP BY p.category']::text[], array['HAVING d.is_weekend']::text[], null, 'Conditions on dimension attributes such as year and is_weekend filter fact rows before grouping, so they belong in WHERE. is_weekend is boolean and can be used directly as a condition.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_date', 'dim_product']::text[]),
(372, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Business Scenarios', 'Month-over-month change. CTE monthly: DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) AS month and SUM(total_amount) AS revenue from orders, GROUP BY 1. Then return month, revenue, and revenue minus the previous month''s (via LAG) AS mom_change.', 'WITH monthly AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) AS month, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY 1) SELECT month, revenue, revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS mom_change FROM monthly', array['WITH monthly AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) AS month, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date)) SELECT month, revenue, revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS mom_change FROM monthly', 'WITH monthly AS (SELECT DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) AS month, SUM(total_amount) AS revenue FROM orders GROUP BY month) SELECT month, revenue, revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) AS mom_change FROM monthly']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One window function reads a value from the previous row.', 'LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) reads the previous row''s revenue, so the difference is the month-over-month change. The first month has no previous row, so its change is NULL.', array['orders']::text[]),
(373, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Data Warehouse', 'Monthly revenue with yearly subtotals and a grand total. Fix the query.', 'SELECT d.year, d.month, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key GROUP BY ROLLUP (d.year, d.month)', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT d.year, d.month, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key GROUP BY ROLLUP d.year, d.month', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'ROLLUP''s columns need to be grouped together.', 'ROLLUP, CUBE and GROUPING SETS take a parenthesized list. ROLLUP (d.year, d.month) returns month rows, a subtotal per year and a grand total.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_date']::text[]),
(374, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'SUM(amount) OVER (ORDER BY order_date) is used as a running total, and two orders share the same date. What do those two rows show?', 'Both show the same total, through that date', '{}'::text[], array['Both show the same total, through that date', 'Each shows its own row-by-row running total', 'The first shows the total, the second NULL', 'Only the first row''s amount is included']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'With ORDER BY and no frame, the default is RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW, which includes all peers with the same date. Use ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW for a strict row-by-row total.', array['orders']::text[]),
(375, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Data Warehouse', 'What does this GROUPING SETS query return?', 'North | 45
Toys | 35
Books | 30
South | 20', '{}'::text[], array['North | 45
Toys | 35
Books | 30
South | 20', 'North | 45
Toys | 35
Books | 30
South | 20
NULL | 65', 'Toys | 35
Books | 30', 'Toys | 35
Books | 20
Books | 10']::text[], 'SELECT COALESCE(category, region) AS grp,
  SUM(revenue) AS rev
FROM sales
GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((category), (region))
ORDER BY rev DESC;', '[{"name":"sales","columns":["category","region","revenue"],"rows":[["Books","North",10],["Books","South",20],["Toys","North",35]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'GROUPING SETS ((category), (region)) is like a UNION ALL of GROUP BY category and GROUP BY region: category totals have NULL region and vice versa. With no () set there is no grand-total row.', '{}'::text[]),
(376, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Business Scenarios', 'Arrange a query showing, for each order, the average of that customer''s current and two previous orders.', 'SELECT customer_id, order_date, total_amount, AVG(total_amount) OVER ( PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW ) AS moving_avg FROM orders', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT customer_id, order_date, total_amount,', 'AVG(total_amount) OVER (', 'PARTITION BY customer_id ORDER BY order_date', 'ROWS BETWEEN 2 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', ') AS moving_avg', 'FROM orders']::text[], array['ROWS BETWEEN 3 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW', 'GROUP BY customer_id']::text[], null, 'PARTITION BY keeps each customer''s orders separate, ORDER BY sets their sequence, and the ROWS frame (2 preceding + current) makes it a 3-order moving average. No GROUP BY is needed: window functions keep every row.', array['orders']::text[]),
(377, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Data Warehouse', 'Product 9 is missing from dim_product. What does this return?', '220 | 150', '{}'::text[], array['220 | 150', '220 | 220', '150 | 150', '220 | NULL']::text[], 'SELECT (SELECT SUM(revenue) FROM fact_sales) AS fact_total,
  SUM(f.revenue) AS joined_total
FROM fact_sales f
JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key;', '[{"name":"fact_sales","columns":["product_key","revenue"],"rows":[[1,100],[2,50],[9,70]]},{"name":"dim_product","columns":["product_key","category"],"rows":[[1,"Books"],[2,"Toys"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The inner join silently drops fact rows whose key has no dimension row, so the joined total loses 70. Warehouses avoid this with an ''Unknown'' dimension member or by checking for orphan keys.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_product']::text[]),
(378, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Business Scenarios', 'Catalogue share per category. From products grouped by category, return category, COUNT(*) AS product_count and ROUND(100.0 * the count / SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (), 2) AS pct.', 'SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS product_count, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) / SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER (), 2) AS pct FROM products GROUP BY category', array['SELECT category, COUNT(*) AS product_count, ROUND(100.0 * COUNT(*) / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM products), 2) AS pct FROM products GROUP BY category']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'OVER () with nothing inside spans the entire grouped result.', 'SUM(COUNT(*)) OVER () adds up the per-category counts across all groups, putting the grand total on every row. Multiplying by 100.0 first forces numeric rather than integer division.', array['products']::text[]),
(379, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'To find streaks of consecutive login days (one row per day), why group by login_date minus its row number in date order?', 'It''s constant inside a run and jumps after a gap', '{}'::text[], array['It''s constant inside a run and jumps after a gap', 'It numbers the streaks 1, 2, 3 in order', 'It removes duplicate dates before counting', 'It equals the length of the current streak']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Inside a run of consecutive days, both the date and the row number go up by 1, so their difference is constant. A gap makes the date jump ahead of the row number, starting a new group (an ''island'').', '{}'::text[]),
(380, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Business Scenarios', 'Funnel: count distinct users who viewed and distinct users who purchased. Fix the query.', 'SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = ''view'') AS viewers, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = ''purchase'') AS buyers FROM user_events', '{}'::text[], null, 'SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = ''view'') AS viewers, COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER WHERE event_type = ''purchase'' AS buyers FROM user_events', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Compare the two FILTER clauses carefully.', 'FILTER''s condition must be in parentheses: agg FILTER (WHERE ...). It lets each aggregate count only matching rows, so every funnel step comes from a single scan.', array['user_events']::text[]),
(381, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Business Scenarios', 'Arrange a query counting new customers per cohort month (the month of their first order).', 'WITH first_orders AS ( SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC(''month'', MIN(order_date)) AS cohort_month FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id ) SELECT cohort_month, COUNT(*) AS new_customers FROM first_orders GROUP BY cohort_month ORDER BY cohort_month', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH first_orders AS (', 'SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC(''month'', MIN(order_date)) AS cohort_month', 'FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id', ')', 'SELECT cohort_month, COUNT(*) AS new_customers', 'FROM first_orders', 'GROUP BY cohort_month', 'ORDER BY cohort_month']::text[], array['SELECT customer_id, DATE_TRUNC(''month'', order_date) AS cohort_month']::text[], null, 'The CTE collapses each customer to their first order month, so every customer lands in exactly one cohort. Truncating every order''s date would put repeat buyers into several months.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(382, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'CTEs', 'In PostgreSQL 12+, a side-effect-free, non-recursive CTE is referenced exactly once. How is it executed by default?', 'Inlined into the outer query, like a subquery', '{}'::text[], array['Inlined into the outer query, like a subquery', 'Materialized first, as an optimization fence', 'Cached and reused by later queries', 'Written to a temporary table on disk']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Since PostgreSQL 12 such CTEs are inlined, so outer filters can be pushed into them; a CTE referenced more than once is materialized by default. MATERIALIZED / NOT MATERIALIZED overrides either choice.', '{}'::text[]),
(383, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Recursive CTEs', 'What path does this query build for Xia?', 'Zoe > Yan > Xia', '{}'::text[], array['Zoe > Yan > Xia', 'Xia > Yan > Zoe', 'Yan > Xia', 'Zoe > Wes > Yan > Xia']::text[], 'WITH RECURSIVE chain AS (
  SELECT id, name, name AS path
  FROM employees WHERE manager_id IS NULL
  UNION ALL
  SELECT e.id, e.name, c.path || '' > '' || e.name
  FROM employees e JOIN chain c ON e.manager_id = c.id
)
SELECT path FROM chain WHERE name = ''Xia'';', '[{"name":"employees","columns":["id","name","manager_id"],"rows":[[1,"Zoe",null],[2,"Yan",1],[3,"Xia",2],[4,"Wes",1]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The path starts at the root and each step appends the child''s name to its manager''s path. Wes is on a different branch, so he never appears in Xia''s path.', array['employees']::text[]),
(384, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Ranking Functions', 'Some employees have a NULL salary. In PostgreSQL, where do they land with RANK() OVER (ORDER BY salary DESC)?', 'First: NULL sorts as larger than any value', '{}'::text[], array['First: NULL sorts as larger than any value', 'Last: NULLs always sort to the end', 'Nowhere: NULL rows get no rank', 'They all receive rank 0']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'PostgreSQL treats NULL as larger than every value, so DESC puts NULLs first by default (ASC puts them last). Use ORDER BY salary DESC NULLS LAST to rank real salaries first.', array['employees']::text[]),
(385, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'CTEs', 'Arrange a single statement that deletes cancelled orders and reports how many rows it removed.', 'WITH removed AS ( DELETE FROM orders WHERE status = ''cancelled'' RETURNING id ) SELECT COUNT(*) AS removed_count FROM removed', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH removed AS (', 'DELETE FROM orders', 'WHERE status = ''cancelled''', 'RETURNING id', ')', 'SELECT COUNT(*) AS removed_count', 'FROM removed']::text[], array['OUTPUT id']::text[], null, 'PostgreSQL allows data-modifying statements inside WITH: the rows listed by RETURNING become the CTE''s result, so the outer query can count them. OUTPUT is SQL Server syntax.', array['orders']::text[]),
(386, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'What happens when PostgreSQL runs this?', 'Returns 1 to 5, since only fetched rows are computed', '{}'::text[], array['Returns 1 to 5, since only fetched rows are computed', 'Loops forever: the CTE has no stop condition', 'Error: a recursive CTE requires a WHERE clause', 'Returns only 1, since LIMIT applies to the anchor']::text[], 'WITH RECURSIVE t AS (
  SELECT 1 AS n
  UNION ALL
  SELECT n + 1 FROM t
)
SELECT n FROM t LIMIT 5;', null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'PostgreSQL evaluates a WITH query only as far as the outer query fetches rows, so LIMIT 5 stops it. The docs warn not to rely on this: an outer sort or join could force full evaluation and loop forever.', '{}'::text[]),
(387, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Ranking Functions', 'How are the bonuses ranked?', 'Bob | 1
Cat | 2
Amy | 3
Dan | 3', '{}'::text[], array['Bob | 1
Cat | 2
Amy | 3
Dan | 3', 'Cat | 1
Amy | 2
Dan | 2
Bob | 4', 'Cat | 1
Amy | 2
Dan | 2
Bob | NULL', 'Cat | 1
Amy | 2
Dan | 2']::text[], 'SELECT name,
  RANK() OVER (ORDER BY bonus DESC) AS r
FROM staff
ORDER BY r, name;', '[{"name":"staff","columns":["name","bonus"],"rows":[["Amy",500],["Bob",null],["Cat",800],["Dan",500]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'PostgreSQL sorts NULL as larger than any value, so with DESC Bob''s NULL bonus comes first and gets rank 1. Add NULLS LAST to the window''s ORDER BY to push it to the bottom.', '{}'::text[]),
(388, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Which of these is NOT allowed in the recursive term of a PostgreSQL recursive CTE?', 'An aggregate such as COUNT(*) over the CTE', '{}'::text[], array['An aggregate such as COUNT(*) over the CTE', 'A JOIN between the CTE and another table', 'A WHERE clause that limits the depth', 'Arithmetic on a column, like level + 1']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'PostgreSQL rejects aggregate functions in the recursive term, and the CTE may be referenced only once there. Aggregate the finished result in the outer query instead.', '{}'::text[]),
(389, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Data Warehouse', 'dim_customer keeps SCD Type 2 history, but the fact is joined on the natural key. What is the result?', 'Consumer | 140
Corporate | 100', '{}'::text[], array['Consumer | 140
Corporate | 100', 'Consumer | 40
Corporate | 100', 'Consumer | 140', 'Consumer | 90
Corporate | 50']::text[], 'SELECT c.segment, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue
FROM fact_sales f
JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_id = c.customer_id
GROUP BY c.segment
ORDER BY c.segment;', '[{"name":"fact_sales","columns":["customer_id","revenue"],"rows":[[7,100],[8,40]]},{"name":"dim_customer","columns":["customer_id","segment","is_current"],"rows":[[7,"Consumer",false],[7,"Corporate",true],[8,"Consumer",true]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'Customer 7 has two versions, so the natural-key join matches both and the 100 sale is counted twice (240 in total instead of 140). Join on the fact''s surrogate key, or filter is_current when you want current attributes.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_customer']::text[]),
(390, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Recursive CTEs', 'Arrange a query that counts orders for every day of 2024-01-01 to 2024-01-07, showing 0 for days without orders.', 'WITH RECURSIVE days AS ( SELECT DATE ''2024-01-01'' AS d UNION ALL SELECT d + 1 FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-01-07'' ) SELECT d, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count FROM days LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.order_date = d GROUP BY d ORDER BY d', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH RECURSIVE days AS (', 'SELECT DATE ''2024-01-01'' AS d', 'UNION ALL', 'SELECT d + 1 FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-01-07''', ')', 'SELECT d, COUNT(o.id) AS order_count', 'FROM days LEFT JOIN orders o ON o.order_date = d', 'GROUP BY d', 'ORDER BY d']::text[], array['SELECT d, COUNT(*) AS order_count', 'FROM days JOIN orders o ON o.order_date = d']::text[], null, 'The date series guarantees one row per day and the LEFT JOIN keeps days without orders. COUNT(o.id) ignores the NULLs of unmatched days, while COUNT(*) would count them as 1.', array['orders']::text[]),
(391, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Ranking Functions', 'Split customers into spend quartiles. From orders, grouped by customer_id, return customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent and NTILE(4) ordered by that sum descending AS quartile.', 'SELECT customer_id, SUM(total_amount) AS spent, NTILE(4) OVER (ORDER BY SUM(total_amount) DESC) AS quartile FROM orders GROUP BY customer_id', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'A window can order by an aggregate; the alias isn''t visible inside OVER.', 'Window functions run after GROUP BY, so OVER (ORDER BY SUM(total_amount) DESC) can order the grouped rows by their totals. The alias spent isn''t visible inside OVER, so the aggregate is repeated.', array['customers', 'orders']::text[]),
(392, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Recursive CTEs', 'This should list each date from 2024-03-01 to 2024-03-07, but PostgreSQL rejects the recursive term''s column type. Fix it so the column stays a date.', 'WITH RECURSIVE days AS (SELECT DATE ''2024-03-01'' AS d UNION ALL SELECT d + 1 FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-03-07'') SELECT d FROM days', array['WITH RECURSIVE days AS (SELECT DATE ''2024-03-01'' AS d UNION ALL SELECT (d + INTERVAL ''1 day'')::date FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-03-07'') SELECT d FROM days', 'WITH RECURSIVE days AS (SELECT DATE ''2024-03-01'' AS d UNION ALL SELECT CAST(d + INTERVAL ''1 day'' AS DATE) FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-03-07'') SELECT d FROM days']::text[], null, 'WITH RECURSIVE days AS (SELECT DATE ''2024-03-01'' AS d UNION ALL SELECT d + INTERVAL ''1 day'' FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-03-07'') SELECT d FROM days', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Compare the anchor column''s type with the type the recursive step produces.', 'date + interval yields a timestamp, but the anchor column is a date, and both terms of a recursive CTE must produce the same type. date + 1 (an integer number of days) keeps it a date.', '{}'::text[]),
(393, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Data Warehouse', 'How many grouping sets does GROUP BY CUBE (region, category, year) produce?', '8', '{}'::text[], array['8', '4', '7', '3']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'CUBE produces every subset of its columns, 2^3 = 8 grouping sets, including the empty set (the grand total). ROLLUP on the same three columns would produce only 4.', '{}'::text[]),
(394, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Recursive CTEs', 'What does this gap-filling query return?', '2024-05-01 | 150
2024-05-02 | 0
2024-05-03 | 80', '{}'::text[], array['2024-05-01 | 150
2024-05-02 | 0
2024-05-03 | 80', '2024-05-01 | 150
2024-05-03 | 80', '2024-05-01 | 150
2024-05-02 | NULL
2024-05-03 | 80', '2024-05-01 | 150
2024-05-02 | 0']::text[], 'WITH RECURSIVE days AS (
  SELECT DATE ''2024-05-01'' AS d
  UNION ALL
  SELECT d + 1 FROM days WHERE d < DATE ''2024-05-03''
)
SELECT d, COALESCE(SUM(s.amount), 0) AS total
FROM days LEFT JOIN sales s ON s.sale_date = d
GROUP BY d
ORDER BY d;', '[{"name":"sales","columns":["sale_date","amount"],"rows":[["2024-05-01",100],["2024-05-01",50],["2024-05-03",80]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'The recursive CTE generates every day including 05-03 (the step runs while d < 05-03, producing 05-03 itself). LEFT JOIN keeps the empty 05-02, and COALESCE turns its NULL sum into 0.', '{}'::text[]),
(395, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Data Warehouse', 'Arrange the query that returns profit per segment and per country in one result, labelling which dimension each row belongs to.', 'SELECT CASE WHEN GROUPING(c.segment) = 0 THEN ''segment'' ELSE ''country'' END AS dim, c.segment, c.country, SUM(f.revenue - f.cost) AS profit FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((c.segment), (c.country))', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['SELECT CASE WHEN GROUPING(c.segment) = 0 THEN ''segment'' ELSE ''country'' END AS dim,', 'c.segment, c.country, SUM(f.revenue - f.cost) AS profit', 'FROM fact_sales f', 'JOIN dim_customer c ON f.customer_key = c.customer_key', 'GROUP BY GROUPING SETS ((c.segment), (c.country))']::text[], array['GROUP BY c.segment, c.country']::text[], null, 'GROUPING SETS computes separate groupings in a single pass. GROUPING(c.segment) is 0 on rows grouped by segment and 1 on rows where segment was aggregated away, which makes a reliable label.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_customer']::text[]),
(396, 'expert', 'multiple-choice', 'Business Scenarios', 'Monthly revenue rows are fed to LAG(revenue, 12) OVER (ORDER BY month) to get last year''s value. When is this wrong?', 'When some months have no row in the data', '{}'::text[], array['When some months have no row in the data', 'When revenue contains decimal values', 'When the data spans more than two years', 'Always, because LAG can only look back one row']::text[], null, null, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'LAG counts rows, not calendar time. Fill in missing months first (e.g. generate_series plus LEFT JOIN) or self-join on month - INTERVAL ''1 year''.', '{}'::text[]),
(397, 'expert', 'write-sql', 'Data Warehouse', 'Revenue cube over category and region: every combination, per-dimension subtotals and a grand total. Alias fact_sales f, dim_product p, dim_store s; join p then s on their keys. Return category, region, SUM(revenue) AS revenue.', 'SELECT p.category, s.region, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON f.product_key = p.product_key JOIN dim_store s ON f.store_key = s.store_key GROUP BY CUBE (p.category, s.region)', array['SELECT p.category, s.region, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_product p ON p.product_key = f.product_key JOIN dim_store s ON s.store_key = f.store_key GROUP BY CUBE (p.category, s.region)']::text[], null, null, null, null, '{}'::text[], 'One GROUP BY option generates every combination of its columns.', 'CUBE (a, b) generates all 2^2 = 4 grouping sets: (a, b), (a), (b) and (). ROLLUP would miss the region-only subtotals because it only drops columns from the right.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_product', 'dim_store']::text[]),
(398, 'expert', 'fix-query', 'Business Scenarios', 'Login streaks: consecutive days are grouped by the date minus the row number, but PostgreSQL says operator does not exist: date - bigint. Fix it.', 'WITH days AS (SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''login'') SELECT user_id, MIN(d) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak FROM (SELECT user_id, d, d - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d)::int AS grp FROM days) t GROUP BY user_id, grp', array['WITH days AS (SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''login'') SELECT user_id, MIN(d) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak FROM (SELECT user_id, d, d - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d)::integer AS grp FROM days) t GROUP BY user_id, grp', 'WITH days AS (SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''login'') SELECT user_id, MIN(d) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak FROM (SELECT user_id, d, d - (ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d))::int AS grp FROM days) t GROUP BY user_id, grp', 'WITH days AS (SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''login'') SELECT user_id, MIN(d) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak FROM (SELECT user_id, d, d - CAST(ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d) AS INT) AS grp FROM days) t GROUP BY user_id, grp']::text[], null, 'WITH days AS (SELECT DISTINCT user_id, event_time::date AS d FROM user_events WHERE event_type = ''login'') SELECT user_id, MIN(d) AS start_day, COUNT(*) AS streak FROM (SELECT user_id, d, d - ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY user_id ORDER BY d) AS grp FROM days) t GROUP BY user_id, grp', null, null, '{}'::text[], 'Check which data type ROW_NUMBER() returns.', 'ROW_NUMBER() returns bigint, and PostgreSQL defines date - integer but not date - bigint. Casting the row number to int yields a date that stays constant within each consecutive streak.', array['user_events']::text[]),
(399, 'expert', 'predict-output', 'Business Scenarios', 'What does this funnel query return?', '3 | 1 | 33.3', '{}'::text[], array['3 | 1 | 33.3', '4 | 1 | 25', '3 | 1 | 0', '3 | 2 | 66.7']::text[], 'WITH f AS (
  SELECT
    COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = ''view'') AS viewers,
    COUNT(DISTINCT user_id) FILTER (WHERE event_type = ''purchase'') AS buyers
  FROM events
)
SELECT viewers, buyers,
  ROUND(100.0 * buyers / viewers, 1) AS conv_pct
FROM f;', '[{"name":"events","columns":["user_id","event_type"],"rows":[[1,"view"],[1,"purchase"],[2,"view"],[2,"view"],[3,"view"],[3,"add_to_cart"]]}]'::jsonb, null, '{}'::text[], null, 'COUNT(DISTINCT ...) counts user 2 once despite two views, giving 3 viewers; only user 1 purchased. Multiplying by 100.0 avoids integer division, so the conversion is 33.3%.', '{}'::text[]),
(400, 'expert', 'drag-drop', 'Business Scenarios', 'Arrange a query showing yearly revenue from the star schema and its year-over-year growth in percent.', 'WITH yearly AS ( SELECT d.year, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key GROUP BY d.year ) SELECT year, revenue, ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year)) / LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year), 1) AS yoy_pct FROM yearly ORDER BY year', '{}'::text[], null, null, null, array['WITH yearly AS (', 'SELECT d.year, SUM(f.revenue) AS revenue', 'FROM fact_sales f JOIN dim_date d ON f.date_key = d.date_key', 'GROUP BY d.year', ')', 'SELECT year, revenue,', 'ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year)) / LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year), 1) AS yoy_pct', 'FROM yearly', 'ORDER BY year']::text[], array['ROUND(100.0 * (revenue - LEAD(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year)) / LEAD(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY year), 1) AS yoy_pct']::text[], null, 'Aggregate to one row per year first, then LAG fetches the previous year''s revenue: growth = (current - previous) / previous. LEAD would compare against the following year instead.', array['fact_sales', 'dim_date']::text[])
